{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-bb7f4fdae8dc25ba", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\t                                        Vorch-Streamer: Extending Human Audio-Visual Generation to Real-Time Long-Form Streaming\n2\t\n3\t\n4\t\n5\t\n6\t                                        Vorch-Streamer: Extending Human Audio-Visual Genera-\n7\t                                        tion to Real-Time Long-Form Streaming\n8\t\n9\t                                        Menglin Han1,2∗ , Yang Ding1∗ , Yulei Lu1 , Haoran Yu1,3 , Xin Ma1 , Junyi Chen1,4 ,\n10\t                                        Zhangkai Ni2† , Lin Ma† , Yaohui Wang1†\n11\t                                        1\n12\t                                            Vorch Team 2 Tongji University 3 Harbin Institute of Technology, Shenzhen 4 Shanghai Jiao Tong University\n13\t\n14\t\n15\t\n16\t\n17\t                                                                          Real-Time Long-Form Audio-Video Joint Generation @ 27 FPS\n18\t                                                                        0s               30s               60s               90s         Last frame (~120s)\n19\t                                              This video shows\n20\t                                             a strong, middle-\n21\t                                               aged white male\n22\t                                              in his thirties,\n23\t                                             against a wooden…\n24\tarXiv:2608.05663v1 [cs.CV] 6 Aug 2026\n25\t\n26\t\n27\t\n28\t\n29\t                                              What it does is it\n30\t                                            causes a prompt drop …\n31\t\n32\t\n33\t                                              Realistic indoor\n34\t                                             kitchen scene. The\n35\t                                            camera is positioned\n36\t                                               in an eye-level\n37\t                                               close-up shot …\n38\t\n39\t                                              Mmm. That tastes so\n40\t                                               damn good. These …\n41\t\n42\t\n43\t                                               Realistic indoor\n44\t                                               vlog-style video\n45\t                                              featuring a young\n46\t                                                 woman sharing\n47\t                                             product recommend…\n48\t\n49\t                                               Actually, I have\n50\t                                            noticed lately that I…\n51\t\n52\t\n53\t\n54\t\n55\t                                        Figure 1: Real-time long-form text-to-audio-video generation with Vorch-Streamer. Conditioned on a\n56\t                                        global caption and long speech (left), our model causally and jointly streams video and synchronized speech\n57\t                                        at 27 FPS, without externally supplied audio or a reference first frame. Frames sampled at 0, 30, 60, and\n58\t                                        90 seconds and near the end of three approximately two-minute rollouts demonstrate sustained identity and\n59\t                                        scene consistency together with expressive facial, gestural, and object motion; the blue waveforms visualize\n60\t                                        the continuously generated audio.\n61\t\n62\t                                                                                               Abstract\n63\t\n64\t                                                      Real-time long-form avatar audio–video generation requires causal, continuous synthesis\n65\t                                                      while maintaining audiovisual synchronization and visual consistency. Adapting a pretrained\n66\t                                                      bidirectional model to this setting presents two key dilemmas. First, autoregressively reusing\n67\t                                                      generated blocks as context creates exposure bias, causing errors and visual drift to accu-\n68\t                                                      mulate over long rollouts. Second, a global speech utterance does not indicates a causal\n69\t                                                      generator which portion should be spoken next when only limited local audio–video context\n70\t                                                      is available. We present Vorch-Streamer, a post-training framework that addresses these\n71\t                                                      challenges and enables real-time long-form Text-to-Audio-Video (T2AV) streaming. We con-\n72\t                                                      struct a synthetic corpus of 80K avatar clips spanning 12–21 seconds and first train a causal\n73\t                                                      generator with mixed Teacher Forcing and Diffusion Forcing. We then apply long-horizon\n74\t                                                      Self Forcing with DMD distillation, exposing the model to its own rollout distribution while\n75\t                                        *Equal contribution †Corresponding author.\n76\t\n77\t\n78\t                                                                                                    1\n79\t\fVorch-Streamer: Extending Human Audio-Visual Generation to Real-Time Long-Form Streaming\n80\t\n81\t\n82\t\n83\t\n84\t        preserving the quality of the pretrained bidirectional teacher. To explicitly control speech\n85\t        progression, an external language model predicts discrete 25-Hz speech-planning tokens,\n86\t        whose continuous features condition the audio diffusion branch and align each causal block\n87\t        with the content it should speak. With bounded causal context and four-step denoising,\n88\t        Vorch-Streamer jointly generates audio and video from text at 27.12 FPS, exceeding the\n89\t        24-FPS real-time playback rate while maintaining competitive audio–lip synchronization\n90\t        and strong identity preservation over long-form generation. Our project page is available at\n91\t        https://vorch-project.github.io/Vorch-Streamer-project/.\n92\t\n93\t\n94\t1   Introduction\n95\tReal-time avatar audio-video generation is a key capability for education, entertainment, virtual communi-\n96\tcation, and interactive agents. Modern audio-video diffusion models support diverse conditioning modes,\n97\tincluding text-, image-, and audio-conditioned generation, and can synthesize visually expressive faces to-\n98\tgether with natural speech (Liu et al., 2024; HaCohen et al., 2026; Seedance et al., 2026). However, most of\n99\tthem are designed for short, offline clips. Their bidirectional attention patterns assume that the complete\n100\tsequence is available during denoising, which is incompatible with a streaming system that must generate\n101\tthe next audio-video segment before future content is observed. Extending these models to long-form real-\n102\ttime generation therefore requires more than reducing inference latency: the model must operate causally,\n103\tpreserve audio-video synchronization over an extended horizon, and remain stable when its own previous\n104\tpredictions become the context for subsequent blocks (Huang et al., 2026c; Yin et al., 2025; Zhu et al., 2026).\n105\tThis setting exposes two related dilemmas. First, long-form streaming requires the model to roll out many\n106\tblocks autoregressively, feeding its own predictions back as context. Small errors can therefore accumulate\n107\tover time, creating a train-test mismatch between clean training conditions and self-generated inference\n108\thistories, i.e., the well-known exposure-bias problem. Under causal attention, this distribution shift can\n109\tcause compounding artifacts, temporal drift, and unstable long-horizon generation. Second, Text-to-Audio-\n110\tVideo (T2AV) exposes a mismatch between global speech conditioning and causal audio-video generation.\n111\tThe text prompt describes the entire utterance, whereas each streaming block can attend only to a limited\n112\twindow of previously generated audio-video content. With bidirectional attention, every token can use\n113\tthe full text and sequence context, but under causal attention each block must infer which portion of the\n114\tutterance it should generate next from limited context alone. This is manageable for short clips but becomes\n115\ta major bottleneck in long-form streaming, especially when the complete transcript is unavailable in advance\n116\tor when speech is supplied in frequently switched short segments. Without explicit speech planning, the\n117\tgenerated audio may start from the middle of the intended speech rather than its beginning, or otherwise\n118\tfall out of temporal alignment with the corresponding speech position.\n119\tWe present Vorch-Streamer, a post-training framework that extends the pretrained LTX2.3 audio-video\n120\tfoundation model to real-time, long-form streaming. Vorch-Streamer separates the transition to causal\n121\tgeneration from the transition to long-horizon generation. We first construct a synthetic corpus of 80K\n122\thigh-quality avatar audio-video clips with durations of 12-21 seconds. Subsequently, a causal autoregressive\n123\tstreaming model is trained using a sample-level mixture of Teacher Forcing and Diffusion Forcing, which\n124\tbehaves as a model capable of 20-step causal short-form streaming with class-free guidance (CFG). The\n125\tresulting short-form causal model is further optimized with long-horizon Self Forcing. In this stage, the\n126\tcausal model performs self-rollouts over 12-21 second horizons with 4-step denoising, while the original\n127\tbidirectional LTX2.3 acts as the teacher for distillation. This combination exposes the student to the\n128\tdistribution it will encounter at inference time and transfers the quality of the pretrained model to long\n129\tcausal trajectories. Together, the causal training and long-horizon distillation transform a bidirectional\n130\tdiffusion model into a stable streaming generator with 4-step causal long-form streaming, which solves the\n131\tfirst dilemma of exposure-bias.\n132\tTo address the second dilemma of speech progression under a limited attention window, Vorch-Streamer\n133\tintroduces an explicit speech-planning pathway based on the Fun-CosyVoice LLM (Lyu et al., 2025). The\n134\tplanner predicts discrete speech-planning tokens, each corresponding to a 40 ms planning unit. We extend\n135\tthe pretrained lookup table (LUT) with a learnable silence token and convert the resulting token IDs into\n136\t\n137\t\n138\t                                                      2\n139\t\fVorch-Streamer: Extending Human Audio-Visual Generation to Real-Time Long-Form Streaming\n140\t\n141\t\n142\t\n143\t\n144\tcontinuous planning features with this LUT. These features are injected into the audio branch through an\n145\tadditional cross-attention branch and adaptively fused with the original text-to-audio cross-attention output\n146\tusing a gated fusion operator. This decoupling of speech planning from audio generation assigns content\n147\tand scheduling to the planner while allowing the generative model to focus on synchronized audio-video\n148\trealization. It also allows an ongoing utterance to be interrupted or switched to new speech at any time\n149\tduring streaming, and the learnable silence token gives the model an explicit silent-listening capability,\n150\tproviding a foundation for interactive applications.\n151\tExperiments show that Vorch-Streamer is the only evaluated native T2AV method to exceed the 24-FPS\n152\treal-time playback rate, while retaining competitive audio–lip synchronization and spoken-content accuracy\n153\tover long-form generation. The results demonstrate that post-training, explicit speech planning, and causal\n154\tinference provide a practical route from a powerful bidirectional foundation model to a real-time text-driven\n155\tavatar generator.\n156\tOur contributions are summarized as follows:\n157\t\n158\t      • We introduce Vorch-Streamer, a post-training framework that adapts a pretrained bidirectional\n159\t        audio-video diffusion model to causal, real-time, long-form streaming generation.\n160\t      • We construct an 80K synthetic avatar audio-video corpus and combine mixed Teacher Forc-\n161\t        ing/Diffusion Forcing with long-horizon Self Forcing and teacher distillation to align causal training\n162\t        with streaming inference.\n163\t      • We propose an LLM-based speech-planning pathway for T2AV that supplies continuous planning\n164\t        features to the audio branch, supports interruption and speech switching through the decoupling of\n165\t        planning and generation, and introduces a learnable silence token for silent listening.\n166\t      • Experiments demonstrate real-time, long-form T2AV streaming at 27.12 FPS, together with com-\n167\t        petitive synchronization, speech accuracy, and long-horizon identity preservation.\n168\t\n169\t2     Related Work\n170\t\n171\t2.1   Joint Audio-Video Generation\n172\t\n173\tEarly audio-visual generation systems commonly synthesize video and audio in a cascade, which does not\n174\tdirectly model their joint distribution and may accumulate semantic and temporal errors across stages.\n175\tEarly text-to-video generation models focus on improving the quality of the generated videos (Wang et al.,\n176\t2024; 2025; Chen et al., 2024; Ma et al., 2025b; 2026). Latte proposed a latent Diffusion Transformer\n177\tarchitecture, laying out a scalable Transformer blueprint that has informed all subsequent video foundation\n178\tmodels (Ma et al., 2025a). Recent text-to-audio-video models instead generate both modalities in a unified\n179\tprocess. Ovi (Low et al., 2025) employs symmetric twin diffusion-transformer backbones with block-wise\n180\tbidirectional cross-modal fusion, while LTX-2 (HaCohen et al., 2026) adopts an asymmetric dual-stream\n181\tarchitecture coupled through bidirectional audio-video cross-attention. Both models can jointly synthesize\n182\tspeech, ambient sound, and corresponding visual events from text, and LTX-2 provides the foundation model\n183\tused by Vorch-Streamer.\n184\tDespite their strong audio-visual quality, these models are designed primarily for bounded offline generation.\n185\tOvi focuses on short clips, while LTX-2 reports temporal drift and degraded synchronization beyond its\n186\tsupported duration. More fundamentally, both of them rely on full-sequence bidirectional denoising, pre-\n187\tventing incremental streaming output and efficient KV-cache reuse. Enabling streaming generation therefore\n188\trequires converting their temporal dependency from bidirectional to causal rather than merely reducing the\n189\tnumber of sampling steps.\n190\t\n191\t2.2   Real-Time and Long-Form Video Generation\n192\t\n193\tReal-time streaming generation requires causal temporal modeling, few-step sampling, and stability under\n194\trepeated conditioning on self-generated history. CausVid and Self Forcing (Yin et al., 2025; Huang et al.,\n195\t\n196\t\n197\t                                                      3\n198\t\fVorch-Streamer: Extending Human Audio-Visual Generation to Real-Time Long-Form Streaming\n199\t\n200\t\n201\t\n202\t\n203\t2026c) establish representative bidirectional-to-causal distillation pipelines, with Self Forcing explicitly re-\n204\tducing the train–test mismatch through autoregressive self-rollout. Causal Forcing (Zhu et al., 2026) further\n205\tidentifies an architectural mismatch when initializing a causal student from a bidirectional teacher and in-\n206\tstead uses an autoregressive teacher for ODE initialization. However, it is a text-to-video method evaluated\n207\ton approximately five-second clips, and does not establish long-horizon or joint audio-video stability.\n208\tLongLive (Yang et al., 2025) combines streaming long tuning, short-window causal attention, a persistent\n209\tframe sink, and KV recaching to support real-time long-form generation and online prompt changes. It\n210\tdemonstrates the importance of train-long–test-long adaptation, but remains a silent T2V model and there-\n211\tfore does not address speech generation or cross-modal synchronization. OmniForcing (Su et al., 2026) is\n212\tthe closest general-purpose precursor for causal joint audio-video generation. It distills the bidirectional\n213\tdual-stream LTX-2 model into a streaming autoregressive generator using asymmetric audio-video block\n214\talignment, audio sink tokens, joint Self-Forcing, and rolling KV caches. However, its quantitative evaluation\n215\tis conducted on short audio-video clips, and it does not study long-form avatar speech, transcript progression,\n216\tor speech interruption.\n217\tVorch-Streamer instead focuses on long-horizon joint generation and explicitly addresses the mismatch be-\n218\ttween a global speech specification and the limited local context available to a causal generator.\n219\t\n220\t\n221\t\n222\t\n223\t2.3   Human-Centric Audio-Video Generation\n224\t\n225\t\n226\tHuman-centric generation additionally requires identity preservation, accurate lip synchronization, expressive\n227\tmotion, and stability throughout long interactions. Live Avatar (Huang et al., 2025) and StreamAvatar (Sun\n228\tet al., 2026) adapt large human video diffusion models into causal streaming generators and introduce\n229\tpersistent reference mechanisms to reduce long-term identity drift. They support long or interactive avatar\n230\trendering, including talking and listening behaviors, but both depend on externally supplied driving audio.\n231\tStreamAvatar further disables text control in its interactive model. Consequently, these methods do not\n232\tjointly determine and synthesize speech, and require an upstream dialogue or speech-generation system.\n233\tHallo-Live (Li et al., 2026a) and StreamChar (Tian et al., 2026) move toward real-time text-driven joint\n234\taudio-video avatars. Hallo-Live introduces asynchronous dual-stream diffusion with limited future audio\n235\tcontext and human-centric preference-guided distillation, but its published evaluation focuses on short-form\n236\tgeneration and leaves longer conversations to future work. StreamChar supports long-form streaming through\n237\tan LLM orchestrator that reads the transcript and audio history, followed by a joint audio-video DiT for local\n238\tdenoising. Its long-form protocol, however, supplies a complete global transcript in advance and does not\n239\treport interrupting or replacing the active speech during generation. It also requires task-specific pretraining\n240\tof the language orchestrator and substantial joint adaptation of a Wan-derived audio-video backbone. Vorch-\n241\tStreamer instead reuses a pretrained speech language model to provide planning features to a post-trained\n242\tcausal backbone, allowing speech to be interrupted or switched without fixing the complete future utterance\n243\tat the beginning of a stream.\n244\tJoyAI-Echo (Li et al., 2026b) uses paired cross-modal memory to preserve appearance and voice consistency\n245\tacross minute-scale multi-shot audio-video stories. Its released pipeline operates on a pre-authored sequence\n246\tof complete shot descriptions, and the public results do not establish online block-wise generation under a\n247\treal-time playback budget. It is therefore better characterized as accelerated multi-shot long-form generation\n248\tthan causal continuous streaming. Wan-Streamer (Huang et al., 2026b;a) takes a broader, natively causal\n249\tapproach by modeling language, audio, and video inputs and outputs in one Transformer, enabling full-duplex\n250\tperception, speaking, listening, and interruption, which is trained as a new end-to-end interactive foundation\n251\tmodel with causal encoders and decoders, broad multimodal pretraining, duplex interaction training, and a\n252\tspecialized serving system. Its public reports emphasize interaction latency but do not provide standardized\n253\tminute-scale generation-quality evaluations. Vorch-Streamer addresses a complementary setting by post-\n254\ttraining an existing high-quality bidirectional audio-video foundation model for causal long-form streaming\n255\twhile retaining its pretrained generative capabilities.\n256\t\n257\t\n258\t                                                       4\n259\t\fVorch-Streamer: Extending Human Audio-Visual Generation to Real-Time Long-Form Streaming\n260\t\n261\t\n262\t\n263\t\n264\t       Stage 1: Synthetic Corpus              Stage 2: Mixed TF/DF Training                     Stage 3: Long-Horizon Self Forcing\n265\t                                                                                                            causal window\n266\t         Human-Centric Prompts             History sequence Denoise sequence                                  KV Cache\n267\t\n268\t\n269\t                                                                          causal                      Long-Horizon Rollout\n270\t       Pretrain T2AV model                     Causal                      attn.\n271\t                                                                                                                      block denoise\n272\t                                                 base                       mask\n273\t                                                model\n274\t          80K synthetic clips\n275\t          12-21s audio-video samples         Flow-matching\n276\t                                                  Loss                                                Long-Horizon DMD Loss\n277\t\n278\t                                              LLM-based Speech Planning\n279\t                            LLM Speech      SP tokens              Embedding LUT      SP embeddings\n280\t          Speech             Planner\n281\t          Utterance\n282\t                                                    Time-aligned clip\n283\t                                                                                                            sigmoid\n284\t                                                                                                             gate\n285\t                                                                                                  s2a\n286\t                                                Audio feature                                   output\n287\t                                                tokens\n288\t                                                                                    s2a attn.\n289\t                                Text\n290\t                              Encoder      Text embedding\n291\t                                               tokens                                                                        refined\n292\t           Global                                                                                 t2a\n293\t           Caption                                                                              output\n294\t                                                                                                              ⊕             t2a output\n295\t                                                                          t2a attn.\n296\t\n297\t\n298\t\n299\t                                         Figure 2: Overview of Vorch-Streamer.\n300\t\n301\t\n302\t3     Method\n303\t\n304\tVorch-Streamer extends the pretrained LTX2.3 audio–video diffusion model to real-time, long-form avatar\n305\taudio–video joint generation. As illustrated in Fig. 2, our method contains three post-training stages.\n306\tWe first construct a large-scale synthetic avatar corpus using the pretrained bidirectional LTX2.3 model.\n307\tSubsequently, a mixed training strategy composed by Diffusion Forcing and Teacher Forcing is adopted to\n308\tconvert the bidirectional model into a block-autoregressive streaming generator. Finally, long-horizon self\n309\tforcing aligns the model with its inference-time rollout distribution under the supervision of the original\n310\tbidirectional model. We additionally use the Fun-CosyVoice LLM as an explicit speech planner, resolving\n311\tthe mismatch between global textual conditioning and causal audio generation by intergrating the planning\n312\tunits into audio branch with cross-attention and gated injection.\n313\t\n314\t3.1   Synthetic Avatar Data\n315\t\n316\tLarge-scale, high-quality, and temporally aligned audio–video data are essential for adapting a foundation\n317\tmodel to streaming avatar generation. We therefore use the pretrained LTX2.3 model to synthesize 80K\n318\thigh-quality avatar audio–video clips with durations ranging from 12 to 21 seconds. Because the samples\n319\tare generated by the same foundation model used for initialization, they provide model-consistent audio–\n320\tvideo supervision for post-training. The resulting corpus is used for both causal streaming training and\n321\tlong-horizon self forcing.\n322\t\n323\t3.2   Causal Audio–Video Streaming\n324\t\n325\tSynchronized block generation. Let x0v and x0a denote clean video and audio latents, respectively.\n326\tVorch-Streamer partitions them into a sequence of synchronized audio–video blocks,\n327\t\n328\t                                              Bb = (x0v [svb : evb ], x0a [sab : eab ]) ,                                                (1)\n329\t\n330\twhere b is the block index. The VAE of LTX2.3 represents video at 3 latent frames per second and audio\n331\tat 25 latent tokens per second. Accordingly, each regular block contains three video latent frames and 25\n332\taudio latent tokens, corresponding to approximately one second of generated content. Due to the causal\n333\t\n334\t\n335\t                                                                  5\n336\t\fVorch-Streamer: Extending Human Audio-Visual Generation to Real-Time Long-Form Streaming\n337\t\n338\t\n339\t\n340\t\n341\tVAE alignment, the first block contains one additional video frame and one additional audio token, following\n342\tOmniForcing (Su et al., 2026).\n343\tWe preserve bidirectional attention within each block to model fine-grained audio–video interactions, while\n344\tenforcing causal attention across blocks. To provide both stable identity context and recent motion context,\n345\tblock b attends to the first three blocks and the most recent three preceding blocks during training,\n346\t\n347\t                                 Hb = {0, 1, 2} ∪ {max(0, b − 3), . . . , b − 1},                           (2)\n348\t\n349\tbut keep adjustable for inference. This bounded global-local window enables constant-memory streaming\n350\twithout discarding the initial appearance and scene information, while maintaining stable memory con-\n351\tsumption. During causal training, the dependency is imposed by causal window attention masks. During\n352\tlong-form training and inference rollout, the same dependency is implemented with a windowed KV cache\n353\tand cache eviction.\n354\t\n355\tMixed Teacher Forcing and Diffusion Forcing. Directly applying Teacher Forcing exposes the model\n356\tonly to clean ground-truth histories, whereas streaming inference conditions on previously generated and\n357\tpotentially imperfect blocks. To reduce this train–test discrepancy, we train the causal model with a sample-\n358\tlevel mixture of Teacher Forcing and Diffusion Forcing. For modality m ∈ {v, a}, a clean latent is corrupted\n359\tthrough flow-matching interpolation,\n360\t\n361\t                                 xσm = (1 − σ)x0m + σϵm ,        ϵm ∼ N (0, I).                             (3)\n362\t\n363\tEach sample is organized as a dual-sequence input containing a history sequence and a denoising sequence\n364\twith same content. For 10% of the samples, the history sequence remains clean, corresponding to Teacher\n365\tForcing. For the remaining 90%, the history sequence is corrupted, corresponding to Diffusion Forcing.\n366\tThe causal mask allows the current denoising block to access preceding history blocks but prevents it from\n367\treading the clean target of the current block, as shown in Fig. 2.\n368\tThe model is optimized with the standard audio–video flow-matching objective,\n369\t                                         X                          2\n370\t                                 LCF =        λm ∥bvm − (ϵm − x0m )∥2 .                                     (4)\n371\t                                           m∈{v,a}\n372\t\n373\t\n374\tThis stage transfers the pretrained model’s joint audio–video generation ability to a causal architecture and\n375\tproduces a short-form streaming model that serves as the initialization for long-horizon training.\n376\t\n377\t3.3   Long-Horizon Self Forcing\n378\t\n379\tAlthough mixed causal forcing makes the model robust to locally corrupted context, it does not fully repro-\n380\tduce the structured errors accumulated during autoregressive inference. Over long sequences, small errors\n381\tin appearance, motion, speech, or synchronization can propagate through the causal history and eventually\n382\tcause severe quality degradation. We address this problem with long-horizon self forcing.\n383\tStarting from the causal model obtained above, the student generates a 12–21 second sequence block by\n384\tblock from its own predictions. Each new block is conditioned on the student’s previously generated blocks\n385\tthrough the same bounded KV-cache window used at inference time. Consequently, training directly exposes\n386\tthe model to its own long-horizon rollout distribution, closing the exposure gap between causal training and\n387\tstreaming inference. The fixed-window cache also prevents memory from growing with sequence length,\n388\tmaking the training behavior consistent with real-time deployment.\n389\tWe retain the original bidirectional LTX2.3 model as a frozen teacher and use distribution matching dis-\n390\ttillation (DMD) (Yin et al., 2024) to preserve the quality and semantic fidelity of the pretrained model.\n391\tThe DMD framework contains three networks with complementary roles: the causal student produces long-\n392\thorizon rollouts; the frozen bidirectional real-score model represents the target distribution of the pretrained\n393\tLTX2.3 model; and a trainable bidirectional fake-score model estimates the evolving distribution of the\n394\tstudent’s generated samples by flow-matching loss.\n395\t\n396\t\n397\t                                                        6\n398\t\fVorch-Streamer: Extending Human Audio-Visual Generation to Real-Time Long-Form Streaming\n399\t\n400\t\n401\t\n402\t\n403\t                        e0m , we first perturb it to a random noise level,\n404\tGiven a student rollout x\n405\t                                           eσm = (1 − σ)e\n406\t                                           x            x0m + σϵm .                                        (5)\n407\tThe same noisy audio–video sample is then evaluated by the real-score and fake-score models,\n408\t                                                       m\n409\t                                     pm                  xσv , x\n410\t                                      real = real_score (e     eσa , c, σ) ,                               (6)\n411\t                                                       m\n412\t                                     pm                  xσv , x\n413\t                                      fake = fake_score (e     eσa , c, σ) .                               (7)\n414\tFor the real-score network, conditional and unconditional predictions are combined to form the guided\n415\tteacher target, while the fake-score network is evaluated on the conditional student distribution. Thus,\n416\tpmreal indicates how the frozen bidirectional teacher would denoise the sample toward the pretrained data\n417\tdistribution, whereas pmfake describes the current student distribution. Their difference provides the DMD\n418\tupdate direction,\n419\t                                              gm ∝ pm        m\n420\t                                                     fake − preal .                                     (8)\n421\tThe causal student is optimized using the corresponding stop-gradient surrogate\n422\t                                          X λm                           2\n423\t                               LDMD =              ∥e         x0m − gm )∥2 ,\n424\t                                                    x0m − sg (e                                            (9)\n425\t                                                 2\n426\t                                          m∈{v,a}\n427\t\n428\twhich moves the student samples toward the real-score prediction and away from the distribution currently\n429\tcaptured by the fake-score model.\n430\tThe fake-score model is updated separately on detached student rollouts. It is trained with the standard\n431\taudio–video flow-matching objective,\n432\t                                         X                             2\n433\t                                                    m\n434\t                               Lfake =        λm ∥bvfake − (ϵm − x\n435\t                                                                 e0m )∥2 .                          (10)\n436\t                                          m∈{v,a}\n437\t\n438\tAlternating the student and fake-score updates continually refreshes the estimate of the student distribution,\n439\tallowing the DMD direction to remain informative as the causal generator improves.\n440\t\n441\t3.4   LLM-based Speech Planning\n442\t\n443\tStreaming audio–video avatar generation introduces an additional challenge for text conditioning. A conven-\n444\ttional text prompt contains the complete speech transcript, but a causal block must determine which portion\n445\tof the transcript should be spoken at the current time. This alignment is implicit in a bidirectional model,\n446\twhere all text, audio, and video tokens can interact globally. Under causal attention, however, the same\n447\tprompt leaves the diffusion model responsible for deciding both what to say and when to say it. The ambi-\n448\tguity becomes particularly problematic in long-form generation and near the boundaries between successive\n449\tspeech segments.\n450\tVorch-Streamer explicitly separates speech planning from audio–video realization. Given the target utter-\n451\tance, the planning LLM predicts a sequence of discrete speech-planning tokens\n452\t                                               z = (z1 , . . . , zTa ),                                  (11)\n453\tat 25 Hz, where each token represents a 40-ms speech-planning unit. The tokens are converted into continuous\n454\tfeatures using the pretrained Fun-CosyVoice lookup table followed by a learnable projection,\n455\t                                             est = fproj (ELUT [zt ]) .                                  (12)\n456\tBecause the planning sequence and the LTX audio latents share the same 25-Hz temporal resolution, each\n457\tcausal block receives the speech features associated with its own time interval.\n458\tWe inject these planning features into the audio diffusion branch through an additional speech cross-attention\n459\tmodule, and strict the audio tokens to only attend the planning features within the same block. Its output\n460\tis adaptively fused with the original text-conditioned audio features using a learnable gate,\n461\t                                 hℓa ← hℓa + sigmoid(gℓ ) Attnℓspeech (hℓa , es ) ,                      (13)\n462\t\n463\t\n464\t                                                          7\n465\t\fVorch-Streamer: Extending Human Audio-Visual Generation to Real-Time Long-Form Streaming\n466\t\n467\t\n468\t\n469\t\n470\twhere hℓa is the audio hidden state and gℓ is the gate in layer ℓ. The original text cross-attention remains\n471\tunchanged and continues to describe global semantics, appearance, and speaking style, while the speech\n472\tbranch supplies an explicit local speaking schedule. This division allows the diffusion model to focus on\n473\tsynchronized audio–video generation rather than inferring speech timing from a global transcript.\n474\tDuring the post-training of Vorch-Streamer, the causal student is conditioned on the global scene description\n475\tand the aligned speech-planning tokens. The bidirectional teacher in Self Forcing still receives prompts\n476\twith full utterance through its original text-conditioning pathway. This asymmetric conditioning allows the\n477\tteacher to retain its pretrained global modeling ability while supervising a student designed for causal speech\n478\texecution.\n479\t\n480\t4     Experiments\n481\t\n482\tWe conduct quantitative and qualitative comparisons against existing joint and cascaded avatar generation\n483\tsystems, evaluating generation quality, audio–video synchronization, speech accuracy, inference speed, and\n484\tstability over approximately two-minute generations. We further ablate the speech-planning module, the\n485\tStage 2 and Stage 3 training strategy, long-horizon Self Forcing, and causal-context selection.\n486\t\n487\t4.1   Experimental Setup\n488\t\n489\tImplementation details. We initialize Vorch-Streamer from the 22B-parameter LTX2.3 model and train\n490\tit on 80K synthetic avatar clips lasting 12–21 seconds at a resolution of 768 × 512 and 24 FPS. Stage 2 uses\n491\t10% Teacher Forcing and 90% Diffusion Forcing and is trained for 6,000 steps on 64 NVIDIA H200 GPUs\n492\twith Fully Sharded Data Parallel (FSDP), bfloat16 mixed precision, gradient checkpointing, and a global\n493\tbatch size of 64 with learning rate 1e − 4. Stage 3 initializes the causal student from the Stage 2 checkpoint\n494\tand performs self-rollouts over the full 12–21-second training horizon. The original bidirectional LTX2.3\n495\tis kept frozen as the real-score teacher for distribution matching distillation. We train Stage 3 for 1,000\n496\titerations on 32 NVIDIA H200 GPUs with a global batch size of 32 and a critic-to-generator update ratio\n497\tof 6:1, with learning rate 1e − 5 for both generator and fake-score model. At inference time, Vorch-Streamer\n498\tuses four denoising steps for each approximately one-second audio–video block. Unless otherwise specified,\n499\tthe causal window uses the 3+1 setting selected by the context ablation: three persistent prefix blocks and\n500\tthe most recent preceding block.\n501\t\n502\tEvaluation protocol. We construct a held-out benchmark containing diverse human identities, languages,\n503\tspeaking styles, backgrounds, and utterance lengths. No prompt in the benchmark appears in the synthetic\n504\ttraining corpus. All comparative evaluations are performed on continuous long-form rollouts targeting ap-\n505\tproximately two minutes. Aggregate visual metrics are computed over the complete available rollout, while\n506\ttime-dependent metrics are sampled at a fixed one-second interval to reveal error accumulation.\n507\tThe T2AV task requires a generator to synthesize both speech and video directly from text. Vorch-Streamer\n508\tis evaluated only in this native T2AV setting. For reference, the “+TTS” baselines are evaluated as TIA2V\n509\tpipelines: they receive speech generated by Qwen3-TTS and a first frame generated by LTX2.3 before video\n510\tsynthesis begins. These results are useful conditional-generation references, but they are not directly task-\n511\tequivalent to from-scratch T2AV generation because the avatar generator is given both the target waveform\n512\tand an appearance anchor. Since many systems lack explicit frame control, determining output length via\n513\taudio or internal mechanisms, we target a nominal two-minute duration. Note that actual lengths may vary.\n514\tOmniForcing and Hallo-Live are limited to 30 seconds due to OOM errors; their temporal curves terminate\n515\tat these respective durations, as indicated in the tables.\n516\t\n517\tBaselines. We compare against the bidirectional foundation model LTX2.3 (HaCohen et al., 2026), the\n518\tjoint audio–video generators JoyAI-Echo (Li et al., 2026b), the streaming avatar model Hallo-Live (Li et al.,\n519\t2026a) and OmniForcing (Su et al., 2026), and two cascaded TIA2V reference pipelines, LiveAvatar (Huang\n520\tet al., 2025)+TTS and SoulX-FlashTalk (Shen et al., 2025)+TTS. LTX2.3 provides a strong offline T2AV\n521\tquality reference but cannot emit blocks causally. JoyAI-Echo, OmniForcing, Hallo-Live, and Vorch-Streamer\n522\tare evaluated in their native joint T2AV setting. LiveAvatar and SoulX-FlashTalk are included only as\n523\t\n524\t\n525\t                                                      8\n526\t\fVorch-Streamer: Extending Human Audio-Visual Generation to Real-Time Long-Form Streaming\n527\t\n528\t\n529\t\n530\t\n531\tTable 1: Quantitative comparison on continuous long-form avatar generation. “Task” distinguishes native\n532\tjoint T2AV generation from TIA2V reference pipelines driven by Qwen3-TTS audio and an LTX2.3-generated\n533\tfirst frame. “Stream” indicates causal block-wise output. Quality metrics are aggregated over the complete\n534\tavailable rollout. Best results are shown in bold and second-best results are underlined; T2AV and TIA2V\n535\tresults should be interpreted under their different input requirements.\n536\t                  Method                   #Param.    Task    Stream   FPS ↑   Sync-C ↑   Sync-D ↓   WER ↓\n537\t                  LTX2.3                     22B      T2AV             1.83      6.80       7.96     7.59%\n538\t                  JoyAI-Echo                 22B      T2AV             3.08      2.44      12.91     9.34%\n539\t                  LiveAvatar+TTS†            14B     TIA2V       ✓     7.58     7.18       8.30      9.21%\n540\t                  SoulX-FlashTalk+TTS†      18.9B    TIA2V       ✓     5.11     8.69       7.26      9.21%\n541\t                  OmniForcing (30 s)         19B      T2AV       ✓     12.12     0.75      12.22     98.79%\n542\t                  Hallo-Live (30 s)         11.7B     T2AV       ✓     11.51     0.58      13.90     94.13%\n543\t                  Vorch-Streamer (ours)     22.8B     T2AV       ✓     27.12     6.62      8.95      7.92%\n544\t †\n545\t  Conditional TIA2V reference using Qwen3-TTS audio and an LTX2.3-generated first frame. Its WER is determined by the shared\n546\t TTS input and does not measure the avatar generator itself.\n547\t\n548\t\n549\t\n550\tconditional references and generate video from the common Qwen3-TTS (Hu et al., 2026) audio and the\n551\tLTX2.3-generated first frame; Vorch-Streamer does not use either auxiliary input.\n552\t\n553\tMetrics. We report DiT throughput in frames per second (FPS), calculated as generated frames divided by\n554\tend-to-end wall-clock time on one NVIDIA H200 GPU. The quantitative comparison employs nine metrics:\n555\tSync-C (↑) and Sync-D (↓) for audio–lip synchronization (Chung & Zisserman, 2016); FID (↓) and FVD\n556\t(↓) (Heusel et al., 2017; Unterthiner et al., 2018) for visual quality; VBench (Huang et al., 2024) Dynamic\n557\tDegree (↑) and Drift (↓); VBench2 (Zheng et al., 2025) Human Anatomy (↑) and Identity (↑); and word\n558\terror rate (WER, ↓). Besides, we assess temporal consistency via ArcFace (Deng et al., 2019) and CLIP\n559\timage similarities (Radford et al., 2021) relative to the initial frame. ArcFace computes cosine similarity on\n560\taligned face embeddings to measure identity preservation, while CLIP uses full-frame embeddings for global\n561\tappearance consistency.\n562\t\n563\t4.2   Quantitative Comparison\n564\t\n565\tEnd-to-end generation quality and efficiency. Table 1 compares Vorch-Streamer with existing joint\n566\tand cascaded avatar generation systems. Vorch-Streamer reaches 27.12 FPS with a 22.8B-parameter pipeline,\n567\tmaking it the only evaluated native T2AV model that exceeds the 24-FPS real-time playback rate in one\n568\tH200 GPU. It is 8.8× faster than JoyAI-Echo, 14.8× faster than bidirectional LTX2.3, approximately 2.2×\n569\tfaster than OmniForcing, and approximately 2.4× faster than Hallo-Live. Among native T2AV methods,\n570\tVorch-Streamer obtains a WER of 7.92%, close to the offline LTX2.3 result of 7.59%. In contrast, directly\n571\textrapolating OmniForcing and Hallo-Live produces WERs of 98.79% and 94.13%, showing that their speech\n572\tlargely deviates from the target transcript. This result reflects the second dilemma: without explicit speech\n573\tplanning, causal blocks cannot reliably determine which part of the global transcript should be spoken\n574\tnext. Vorch-Streamer’s speech planner preserves transcript accuracy over the approximately two-minute\n575\tgeneration. Its Sync-C/Sync-D scores of 6.62/8.95 are also close to LTX2.3 at 6.80/7.96, showing that\n576\tfour-step causal inference preserves most of the synchronization and speech accuracy of the much slower\n577\tbidirectional model.\n578\tThe TIA2V references achieve strong synchronization on some metrics, particularly SoulX-FlashTalk+TTS\n579\twith Sync-C 8.69 and Sync-D 7.26. However, this advantage comes from a substantially easier input setting:\n580\tboth TIA2V pipelines are supplied with the complete target audio and a generated first frame, so they\n581\tneither generate the audiovisual content from scratch nor determine speech progression from text. They also\n582\trun at only 7.58 and 5.11 FPS in one GPU, respectively, both far below real-time playback and 3.6×–5.3×\n583\tslower than Vorch-Streamer. Their WER is inherited from the shared Qwen3-TTS input and therefore does\n584\tnot measure the avatar generator. More importantly, external audio bypasses rather than solves the second\n585\tdilemma: the pipeline no longer has to align a global text instruction with the locally available causal audio\n586\tcontext.\n587\t\n588\t\n589\t                                                             9\n590\t\fVorch-Streamer: Extending Human Audio-Visual Generation to Real-Time Long-Form Streaming\n591\t\n592\t\n593\t\n594\t\n595\tTable 2: Visual quality and human-centric fidelity over continuous long-form rollouts. Scores are aggregated\n596\tuniformly over temporal positions in the complete available output.\n597\t  Method                  FID ↓   FVD ↓ Dynamic ↑ Drift ↓ Human Anatomy ↑ Human Identity ↑ ArcFace ↑ CLIP I.Sim. ↑\n598\t  LTX2.3                  115.12 521.74     0.2941      0.0776        0.9680             0.8733         0.5679       0.8866\n599\t  JoyAI-Echo              110.81 354.85     0.0471      0.0481        0.9710             0.9789         0.6777       0.9075\n600\t  LiveAvatar+TTS      120.94      457.85    0.2824    0.0301          0.9651             0.9722         0.7387       0.9532\n601\t  SoulX-FlashTalk+TTS 123.13      448.33    0.2000    0.0231          0.9719             0.9849         0.8431       0.9552\n602\t  OmniForcing (30 s)      167.26 1217.10    0.1529      0.0663        0.8884            0.8012          0.3870       0.8804\n603\t  Hallo-Live (30 s)       143.18 1007.90    0.0000      0.0811        0.9291            0.8670          0.4652       0.8752\n604\t  Vorch-Streamer (ours)   119.80 549.99     0.2706      0.0286        0.9233            0.9996          0.7534       0.9324\n605\t\n606\t\n607\t\n608\tTable 3: Long-horizon preservation over each method’s available rollout. We report the score over the final\n609\t10-second window and, in parentheses, its change from the initial 10-second window.\n610\tMethod                    Sync-C ↑         Sync-D ↓          Dynamic ↑             Drift ↓        Human Anatomy ↑ Human Identity ↑\n611\tLTX2.3              6.21 (−18.51%) 8.46 (+14.98%) 0.1647 (−61.11%) 0.0336 (−32.93%)                0.9589 (−1.21%)   0.9595 (+0.07%)\n612\tLiveAvatar+TTS      7.04 (−3.57%)   8.38 (+2.80%)   0.2941 (−3.85%) 0.0234 (−17.54%)               0.9707 (+0.32%)   0.9960 (+1.12%)\n613\tSoulX-FlashTalk+TTS 8.79 (+1.65%)   7.25 (−1.55%)   0.2118 (+28.57%) 0.0202 (−8.99%)               0.9690 (+0.16%)   0.9956 (+1.22%)\n614\tJoyAI-Echo          2.24 (−16.30%) 12.90 (+0.11%) 0.0353 (−50.00%) 0.0355 (+68.78%)                0.9868 (+2.19%)   0.9874 (−1.13%)\n615\tOmniForcing (30 s)  0.46 (−31.94%) 13.52 (+2.40%) 0.2235 (+280.00%) 0.0096 (−78.35%)               0.9117 (+3.44%)   1.0000 (+13.04%)\n616\tHallo-Live (30 s)   0.29 (−53.94%) 15.58 (+12.05%) 0.0000 (−100.00%) 0.0243 (−30.41%)              0.9000 (−3.76%)   0.9633 (−0.70%)\n617\tVorch-Streamer (ours)   6.67 (−0.49%)   8.88 (+0.47%)     0.2588 (+10.00%)     0.0221 (−5.48%)     0.9284 (−0.04%)   1.0000 (+0.06%)\n618\t\n619\t\n620\t\n621\t\n622\tVisual quality and human fidelity. Table 2 reports perceptual quality and human fidelity aggregated\n623\tover each method’s complete available long-form rollout. Vorch-Streamer achieves the highest Human Iden-\n624\ttity score (0.9996), the second-highest Dynamic Degree among native T2AV methods (0.2706), and the\n625\tsecond-lowest Drift overall (0.0286), while maintaining competitive FID and FVD. SoulX-FlashTalk obtains\n626\thigher ArcFace similarity, and both TIA2V references obtain higher CLIP similarity. This is unsurprising\n627\tbecause their first-frame condition directly fixes the target identity and scene appearance. The conditional\n628\tadvantage should not be interpreted as stronger from-scratch generation: Vorch-Streamer must create the\n629\tinitial person, voice, motion, and scene jointly from text and then preserve them causally. Compared with\n630\tthe native streaming T2AV baselines, Vorch-Streamer is markedly stronger in FID, FVD, dynamics, drift,\n631\tidentity, ArcFace, and CLIP similarity, while its anatomy score remains comparable; OmniForcing and\n632\tHallo-Live are additionally evaluated only up to 30 seconds.\n633\t\n634\t4.3   Long-Horizon Analysis\n635\t\n636\tWe evaluate long-horizon behavior from both endpoint changes and complete temporal trajectories. Table 3\n637\treports each metric over the final 10-second window together with its relative change from the initial 10\n638\tseconds. Vorch-Streamer exhibits little endpoint degradation over its approximately two-minute rollout.\n639\tMeanwhile, its Dynamic Degree remains high at 0.2588 and increases by 10.00%, showing that the stable\n640\tappearance is not achieved by producing a static video. In comparison, the native T2AV baselines show\n641\tsubstantially larger long-horizon changes. LTX2.3 loses 18.51% in Sync-C and 61.11% in Dynamic Degree,\n642\tand JoyAI-Echo loses 16.30% and 50.00%, respectively, while its Drift increases by 68.78%. OmniForcing\n643\tand Hallo-Live already suffer marked synchronization degradation within their shorter 30-second outputs,\n644\twith Hallo-Live also losing all measured dynamics. The TIA2V references preserve strong endpoint metrics,\n645\tbut they are conditioned on both external audio and a first frame and therefore serve as conditional refer-\n646\tences rather than like-for-like T2AV comparisons. Overall, Table 3 shows that Vorch-Streamer provides the\n647\tstrongest balance of speech synchronization, sustained motion, and long-term preservation among the native\n648\tstreaming T2AV methods.\n649\tFig. 3 complements the endpoint comparison by showing when appearance degradation occurs. For each\n650\tvideo, we compute ArcFace and CLIP Image cosine similarities between the first frame and the frame sam-\n651\tpled at each subsequent second, and then average the independently computed trajectories across benchmark\n652\tsamples; the curves begin at t = 1 second, show 95% confidence intervals, and terminate at each method’s ac-\n653\ttual maximum duration without extrapolation. Vorch-Streamer remains stable throughout its approximately\n654\t\n655\t\n656\t                                                                 10\n657\t\fVorch-Streamer: Extending Human Audio-Visual Generation to Real-Time Long-Form Streaming\n658\t\n659\t\n660\t\n661\t\n662\t                                   ArcFace similarity to the first frame                                                                                   CLIP Image similarity to the first frame\n663\t                 1.0                                                                                                      1.00\n664\t\n665\t\n666\t                                                                                                                      0.95\n667\t\n668\t                 0.8\n669\t                                                                                                                      0.90\n670\t  ＞土」e-\n671\t\n672\t\n673\t\n674\t\n675\t                                                                                                            >1\n676\t                                                                                                                          0\n677\t                                                                                                                              郖\n678\t                                                                                                             le-\n679\t                 0\n680\t                 6\n681\t\n682\t\n683\t\n684\t\n685\t                                                                                                            」  l\n686\t      lE1\n687\t\n688\t\n689\t\n690\t\n691\t                                                                                                                E1\n692\t                                                                                                                          0\n693\t                                                                                                                          80 75\n694\t        sau1\n695\t\n696\t\n697\t\n698\t\n699\t                                                                                                                 sau1\n700\t                 0\n701\t                 4\n702\t           s0u\n703\t\n704\t\n705\t\n706\t\n707\t                                                                                                                    sou\n708\t                                                                                                                             0\n709\t                                                                                                                      0.70\n710\t                 0.2\n711\t                                                                                                                      0.65\n712\t\n713\t\n714\t                 0.0                                                                                                  0.60\n715\t                            20     40             60                80           100            120                                             20            40              60                80    100   120\n716\t                                          Time from first frame (s)                                                                                                   Time from first frame (s)\n717\t\n718\t                                                                         一   Hal lo-Live   一   LTX23           一              OmniForcing        一   Vorch-Streamer\n719\t                                                                         —   丿oyAI-Ech。    —   LiveAvatar      —              SoulX-FlashTalk\n720\t\n721\t\n722\t\n723\t\n724\tFigure 3: Long-horizon consistency as a function of generation time. The two panels show ArcFace similarity\n725\tand CLIP Image similarity to the first frame of the same video. ArcFace measures facial-identity preservation,\n726\twhereas CLIP Image measures broader appearance and scene preservation. Solid lines denote the mean\n727\tacross benchmark samples and shaded regions denote 95% confidence intervals. Curves terminate at the\n728\tmaximum duration completed by each method and are never extrapolated. VBench Dynamic Degree is\n729\treported separately in Table 2 to rule out artificially high similarity from static outputs.\n730\t\n731\t\n732\t\n733\t\n734\ttwo-minute rollout: after the initial transition, ArcFace similarity stays around 0.73–0.77 and CLIP Image\n735\tsimilarity around 0.92–0.94, with no sustained downward trend. In contrast, the native T2AV methods that\n736\tcomplete long rollouts degrade continuously: LTX2.3 falls to roughly 0.42 ArcFace and 0.82 CLIP similarity,\n737\twhile JoyAI-Echo falls to roughly 0.62 and 0.88, respectively. OmniForcing and Hallo-Live also decline before\n738\tterminating at 30 seconds. The TIA2V curves remain strong, particularly for SoulX-FlashTalk, but reflect\n739\tthe additional identity and content anchors supplied by their first-frame and audio conditions. Together, the\n740\tendpoint metrics and temporal curves show that Vorch-Streamer maintains identity and scene appearance\n741\tthroughout long native T2AV generation rather than merely delaying degradation beyond a short evaluation\n742\twindow.\n743\t\n744\t\n745\t\n746\t\n747\t4.4                    Qualitative Comparison\n748\t\n749\t\n750\tFig. 4 compares two long-form prompts at the beginning, middle, and end of each available rollout. Vorch-\n751\tStreamer preserves the generated subject’s facial structure, clothing, and background over 120 and 113\n752\tseconds, while still changing gaze, expression, hand pose, and object orientation. This combination is\n753\timportant: a nearly static video can score well on appearance similarity without demonstrating useful long-\n754\tform generation, whereas the Vorch-Streamer samples remain visibly dynamic.\n755\tThe native T2AV baselines exhibit different limitations. LTX2.3 develops severe facial and background\n756\tcorruption near the end, while Hallo-Live already contains strong color and structural artifacts by 30 seconds.\n757\tJoyAI-Echo remains visually coherent and includes a clear hand–object interaction in the second example,\n758\tbut its left sequence shows relatively little pose variation, while exhibit noticeable degradation in faces.\n759\tOmniForcing exhibits noticeable frame freezing and substantial visual quality degradation within just 30\n760\tseconds of generation, indicating poor temporal stability even over relatively short horizons. The TIA2V\n761\trows preserve identity more reliably, but they begin from the same externally generated first frame and follow\n762\texternally supplied audio. Their visual stability therefore reflects strong conditional animation rather than\n763\tfrom-scratch audiovisual generation, and it does not demonstrate a solution to text-to-speech progression\n764\tunder a causal context. Overall, the qualitative comparison supports the quantitative finding that Vorch-\n765\tStreamer offers a better balance of long-horizon stability, motion, and real-time native T2AV generation.\n766\t\n767\t\n768\t                                                                                                      11\n769\t\fVorch-Streamer: Extending Human Audio-Visual Generation to Real-Time Long-Form Streaming\n770\t\n771\t\n772\t\n773\t\n774\t                     1s            60s             120s              1s              60s             113s\n775\t\n776\t\n777\t      LTX2.3\n778\t\n779\t\n780\t\n781\t\n782\t  JoyAI-Echo\n783\t\n784\t\n785\t\n786\t\n787\t   LiveAvatar\n788\t\n789\t\n790\t\n791\t\n792\t       SoulX-\n793\t      FlashTalk\n794\t\n795\t\n796\t\n797\t   Hallo-Live\n798\t  (1 / 15 / 30s)\n799\t\n800\t\n801\t\n802\t  OmniForcing\n803\t  (1 / 15 / 30s)\n804\t\n805\t\n806\t\n807\t Vorch-Streamer\n808\t     (Ours)\n809\t\n810\t\n811\t\n812\tFigure 4: Qualitative comparison on two long-form T2AV prompts. We show frames near the beginning,\n813\tmiddle, and end of each available rollout; OmniForcing and Hallo-Live are limited to 30 seconds and are\n814\ttherefore sampled at 1, 15, and 30 seconds. LiveAvatar and SoulX-FlashTalk are TIA2V references condi-\n815\ttioned on Qwen3-TTS audio and an LTX2.3-generated first frame, whereas all other rows synthesize audio\n816\tand video directly from text. Vorch-Streamer maintains coherent identity and scene structure throughout\n817\tthe full sequence while retaining visible facial and upper-body motion.\n818\t\n819\t\n820\t4.5      Ablation Study\n821\t\n822\t4.5.1      Effect of Speech Planning\n823\t\n824\tWe compare the proposed speech-planning features with three alternatives that expose text to the audio\n825\tgenerator with different degrees of temporal localization. As shown in Table 4, conditioning every block on\n826\tthe complete utterance produces an extremely high WER of 184%. The same global transcript is visible\n827\tthroughout generation, but the causal generator has no reliable signal indicating which part should be spoken\n828\tin the current block, directly exposing the second dilemma described in the Introduction. In practice, it\n829\tgenerates unnaturally fast and incorrect speech as it attempts to realize the long utterance from limited local\n830\taudio context. Splitting the utterance into short text segments reduces the visible attention and lowers WER\n831\tto 62.77%, since the generator only needs to select content from a small piece of text. However, when the\n832\tcondition switches to a new segment, the cached audio context still corresponds to the preceding segment\n833\tand is inconsistent with the new text. This mismatch can make generation enter the new segment from\n834\tan intermediate position and can also cause repeated speech. Assigning a timestamp to every text token,\n835\tencoding it with RoPE, and restricting each audio token to its corresponding text window further alleviates\n836\tthis boundary ambiguity. However, consecutive word repetitions still occur with non-negligible probability,\n837\tintroducing insertion errors and keeping WER relatively high.These designs do not fundamentally resolve the\n838\t\n839\t\n840\t                                                      12\n841\t\fVorch-Streamer: Extending Human Audio-Visual Generation to Real-Time Long-Form Streaming\n842\t\n843\t\n844\t\n845\t\n846\tTable 4: Ablation of speech planning for T2AV. WER measures spoken-content accuracy, while Sync-C and\n847\tSync-D measure audio–lip synchronization.\n848\t                            Speech condition          WER ↓ Sync-C ↑ Sync-D ↓\n849\t                            Complete long utterance 184%          5.58      9.66\n850\t                            Segmented short text    62.77%        6.76      8.98\n851\t                            Window text RoPE        65.46%        4.75      9.19\n852\t                            LLM speech plan (ours)   7.92%        6.62      8.95\n853\t\n854\t\n855\t\n856\tTable 5: Ablation of Stage 2 and Stage 3 on continuous long-form rollouts. Without Stage 2, the bidirectional\n857\tDiT is first flow-matching fine-tuned to use the introduced LLM planner, after which causal audio–video\n858\tforcing is skipped and Stage 3 is applied. Without Stage 3, the Stage 2 checkpoint is evaluated using 20-step\n859\tCFG denoising; the full model uses four denoising steps per block. Dynamic Degree and Drift are computed\n860\twith VBench, Human Identity with VBench2, and WER measures spoken-content accuracy.\n861\t   Variant                         FVD ↓ Sync-C ↑ Sync-D ↓ Dynamic ↑ Drift ↓ Human Identity ↑ WER ↓\n862\t   Without Stage 2               515.30        6.89    8.73      0.0824    0.0238       1.0000       11.16%\n863\t   Without Stage 3 (20-step CFG) 442.99        6.07    9.18      0.3059    0.0312       0.9939       9.17%\n864\t   Stage 2 + Stage 3 (ours)      549.99        6.62    8.95      0.2706    0.0286       0.9996       7.92%\n865\t\n866\t\n867\t\n868\tsecond dilemma. In contrast, the LLM planner converts the utterance into an explicit block-aligned sequence\n869\tof speech units, reducing WER to 7.92% while retaining synchronization comparable to other conditioning\n870\tstrategies. These results show that temporal restrictions on text attention are helpful, but explicitly planning\n871\twhat should be spoken at each time step is crucial for accurate causal T2AV generation.\n872\t\n873\t4.5.2   Effect of Stage 2 and Stage 3\n874\t\n875\tWe ablate Stage 2 and Stage 3 to isolate the necessity of causal initialization before long-horizon Self Forcing\n876\tand the benefit of adapting the resulting causal generator to efficient few-step inference. To ensure that the\n877\tspeech-planning condition is effective in a fair “Without Stage 2” comparison, we first introduce the LLM\n878\tplanner and fine-tune the bidirectional DiT with the flow-matching objective. We then skip the causal audio–\n879\tvideo forcing in Stage 2 and initialize Stage 3 from this planner-adapted bidirectional checkpoint. Table 5\n880\tshows that Stage 2 is essential for establishing a causal generator with meaningful motion before long-horizon\n881\tSelf Forcing. Applying Stage 3 without this causal initialization reduces Dynamic Degree from 0.2706 to\n882\t0.0824, while WER increases slightly from 7.92% to 11.16%. Although this variant obtains comparable\n883\tsynchronization and seemingly favorable Drift and Human Identity scores, the temporal-preservation metrics\n884\tmust be interpreted together with its severe loss of dynamics: a low-motion rollout is less likely to accumulate\n885\tvisual drift or identity changes. Stage 2 therefore provides more than adaptation to the speech planner; it\n886\tpreserves the motion-generation capability while converting the bidirectional model into a causal generator\n887\tsuitable for meaningful long-form rollout. Stage 3 plays a complementary role. Relative to the Stage 2\n888\tcheckpoint evaluated with 20-step CFG, it enables four-step inference while improving Sync-C/Sync-D from\n889\t6.07/9.18 to 6.62/8.95, WER from 9.17% to 7.92%, Drift from 0.0312 to 0.0286, and Human Identity from\n890\t0.9939 to 0.9996. Together, the two stages prevent motion collapse while adapting the causal generator to\n891\taccurate and efficient few-step long-form inference.\n892\t\n893\t4.5.3   Effect of Long-Horizon Self Forcing\n894\t\n895\tTo isolate the benefit of training on complete causal trajectories, we compare Stage 2 alone with self forcing\n896\trestricted to the first five, a random five, or the last five blocks, as well as the proposed full-horizon self\n897\tforcing. Restricting the objective to a small part of the rollout produces unbalanced behavior. Training\n898\ton the first five blocks yields the highest Dynamic Degree (0.5059), but also substantially increases Drift\n899\tto 0.0739 and reduces Human Identity to 0.9021, indicating that the additional motion is accompanied by\n900\taccumulated appearance changes. Supervising only the last five blocks is even less stable, with the highest\n901\t\n902\t\n903\t                                                      13\n904\t\fVorch-Streamer: Extending Human Audio-Visual Generation to Real-Time Long-Form Streaming\n905\t\n906\t\n907\t\n908\t\n909\tTable 6: Ablation of long-horizon self forcing. FVD, VBench Dynamic Degree and Drift, and VBench2\n910\tHuman Identity are computed over the available rollout. ArcFace and CLIP-I report the mean similarity of\n911\tthe final 10-second window to the first frame.\n912\t         Training horizon              FVD ↓ Dynamic ↑ Drift ↓ Human Identity ↑ ArcFace ↑ CLIP-I ↑\n913\t         Stage 2 only (20-step CFG)    442.99    0.3059    0.0312       0.9939            0.7478     0.9445\n914\t         First five blocks             556.32    0.5059    0.0739       0.9021            0.5800     0.8757\n915\t         Random five blocks            461.95    0.1647    0.0698       0.9878            0.6869     0.8861\n916\t         Last five blocks              443.87    0.2000    0.1368       0.6995            0.4437     0.7895\n917\t         Full horizon (ours)           549.99    0.2706    0.0286       0.9996            0.7534     0.9324\n918\t\n919\t\n920\tTable 7: Inference-time ablation of causal-context selection. Following Table 6, FVD, VBench Dynamic\n921\tDegree and Drift, and VBench2 Human Identity are computed over the available rollout. ArcFace and\n922\tCLIP-I report the mean similarity of the final 10-second window to the first frame.\n923\t                 Context      FVD ↓ Dynamic ↑ Drift ↓ Human Identity ↑ ArcFace ↑ CLIP-I ↑\n924\t                 1+3          544.13    0.2941    0.0383       0.9328            0.5882     0.9082\n925\t                 0+3          501.70    0.2235    0.1589       0.6921            0.2759     0.7533\n926\t                 3+3          498.93    0.2706    0.0377       0.9427            0.6386     0.9150\n927\t                 3+1 (ours)   549.99    0.2706    0.0286       0.9996            0.7534     0.9324\n928\t\n929\t\n930\t\n931\tDrift (0.1368) and the lowest identity and final-window similarities, because the late blocks must recover from\n932\tearlier self-generated states that were not directly optimized. Random-five-block training covers more varied\n933\tstates, but its Drift (0.0698) remains more than twice that of full-horizon training. Full-horizon self forcing\n934\tachieves the lowest Drift (0.0286), the highest Human Identity (0.9996), and the highest ArcFace similarity\n935\t(0.7534). Although it does not minimize FVD or maximize Dynamic Degree, it provides the strongest overall\n936\tlong-horizon preservation, supporting the need to optimize the complete inference trajectory rather than a\n937\tsparse subset of rollout positions.\n938\t\n939\t4.5.4   Impact of Context Selection\n940\t\n941\tWe evaluate context selection as an inference-time ablation, keeping the model fixed and varying only the\n942\tcausal attention window. We denote a window by P +R, where P is the number of persistent prefix blocks\n943\tand R is the number of recent blocks; the Stage 2 checkpoint with 20-step CFG in Table 6 is reported only as a\n944\treference. The 3+1 setting is comparable to the other context variants in metrics such as FVD and Dynamic\n945\tDegree, but its necessity becomes clear over long rollouts. All other settings exhibit varying degrees of\n946\tdegradation, most clearly in Drift, ArcFace, and CLIP-I. Without a persistent prefix, 0+3 degrades severely\n947\tto 0.1589 Drift, 0.2759 ArcFace, and 0.7533 CLIP-I. Retaining prefix context in 1+3 and 3+3 alleviates the\n948\tfailure, but their Drift remains higher at 0.0383 and 0.0377, respectively, and their ArcFace/CLIP-I scores\n949\tremain lower at 0.5882/0.9082 and 0.6386/0.9150. In contrast, 3+1 achieves the lowest Drift of 0.0286 and the\n950\thighest ArcFace and CLIP-I scores of 0.7534 and 0.9324 among the tested context windows. These results\n951\tmake the 3+1 context necessary for reliable long-video inference: three persistent prefix blocks maintain\n952\tglobal appearance anchors, while using only the immediately preceding block as recent context limits the\n953\taccumulation of errors from self-generated history. Its advantage lies primarily in long-horizon preservation\n954\trather than uniformly improving the other quality metrics.\n955\t\n956\t5   Conclusion\n957\t\n958\tWe presented Vorch-Streamer, a post-training framework that transforms a pretrained bidirectional audio–\n959\tvideo diffusion model into a causal generator for real-time, long-form text-to-audio-video avatar generation.\n960\tCausal audio–video forcing provides a stable initialization for synchronized block-wise generation, and long-\n961\thorizon self forcing exposes the model to its own rollout distribution while using the frozen bidirectional\n962\tmodel to preserve generation quality. An LLM-based speech planner aligns global textual content with the\n963\t\n964\t\n965\t                                                          14\n966\t\fVorch-Streamer: Extending Human Audio-Visual Generation to Real-Time Long-Form Streaming\n967\t\n968\t\n969\t\n970\t\n971\tcausal audio timeline, improving the model’s ability to determine what should be spoken in each block. To-\n972\tgether with a bounded global–local context and four-step block denoising, these components enable efficient\n973\tstreaming without allowing memory consumption to grow with sequence length. Experiments on continu-\n974\tous, approximately two-minute generations show that Vorch-Streamer reaches 27.12 FPS while maintaining\n975\tcompetitive visual quality, audio–lip synchronization, speech accuracy, and strong long-term identity preser-\n976\tvation. Unlike the conditional TIA2V references, it requires neither an input audio track nor a first frame and\n977\tdirectly addresses speech progression from text under causal generation. These results establish causal forcing\n978\tand inference-aligned long-horizon training as a practical route for adapting large bidirectional audio–video\n979\tfoundation models to interactive avatar generation.\n980\t\n981\tReferences\n982\tXinyuan Chen, Yaohui Wang, Lingjun Zhang, Shaobin Zhuang, Xin Ma, Jiashuo Yu, Yali Wang, Dahua\n983\t  Lin, Yu Qiao, and Ziwei Liu. Seine: Short-to-long video diffusion model for generative transition and\n984\t  prediction. In International Conference on Learning Representations, 2024.\n985\t\n986\tJoon Son Chung and Andrew Zisserman. Out of time: automated lip sync in the wild. In Asian conference\n987\t  on computer vision, pp. 251–263. Springer, 2016.\n988\t\n989\tJiankang Deng, Jia Guo, Niannan Xue, and Stefanos Zafeiriou. Arcface: Additive angular margin loss\n990\t  for deep face recognition. In Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF conference on computer vision and pattern\n991\t  recognition, pp. 4690–4699, 2019.\n992\t\n993\tYoav HaCohen, Benny Brazowski, Nisan Chiprut, Yaki Bitterman, Andrew Kvochko, Avishai Berkowitz,\n994\t  Daniel Shalem, Daphna Lifschitz, Dudu Moshe, Eitan Porat, et al. Ltx-2: Efficient joint audio-visual\n995\t  foundation model. arXiv preprint arXiv:2601.03233, 2026.\n996\t\n997\tMartin Heusel, Hubert Ramsauer, Thomas Unterthiner, Bernhard Nessler, and Sepp Hochreiter. Gans\n998\t trained by a two time-scale update rule converge to a local nash equilibrium. Advances in neural infor-\n999\t mation processing systems, 30, 2017.\n1000\t\n1001\tHangrui Hu, Xinfa Zhu, Ting He, Dake Guo, Bin Zhang, Xiong Wang, Zhifang Guo, Ziyue Jiang, Hongkun\n1002\t  Hao, Zishan Guo, et al. Qwen3-tts technical report. arXiv preprint arXiv:2601.15621, 2026.\n1003\t\n1004\tLianghua Huang, Zhi-Fan Wu, Yupeng Shi, Wei Wang, Mengyang Feng, Junjie He, Chen-Wei Xie, Yu Liu,\n1005\t  Jingren Zhou, Ang Wang, et al. Wan-streamer v0. 2: Higher resolution, same latency. arXiv preprint\n1006\t  arXiv:2607.04443, 2026a.\n1007\t\n1008\tLianghua Huang, Zhifan Wu, Wei Wang, Yupeng Shi, Mengyang Feng, Junjie He, Chenwei Xie, Yu Liu,\n1009\t  Jingren Zhou, Ang Wang, et al. Wan-streamer v0. 1: End-to-end real-time interactive foundation models.\n1010\t  arXiv preprint arXiv:2606.25041, 2026b.\n1011\t\n1012\tXun Huang, Zhengqi Li, Guande He, Mingyuan Zhou, and Eli Shechtman. Self forcing: Bridging the\n1013\t train-test gap in autoregressive video diffusion. Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems, 38:\n1014\t 167283–167308, 2026c.\n1015\t\n1016\tYubo Huang, Hailong Guo, Fangtai Wu, Weiqiang Wang, Shifeng Zhang, Shijie Huang, Qijun Gan, Lin Liu,\n1017\t  Sirui Zhao, Enhong Chen, et al. Live avatar: Streaming real-time audio-driven avatar generation with\n1018\t  infinite length. arXiv preprint arXiv:2512.04677, 2025.\n1019\t\n1020\tZiqi Huang, Yinan He, Jiashuo Yu, Fan Zhang, Chenyang Si, Yuming Jiang, Yuanhan Zhang, Tianxing Wu,\n1021\t  Qingyang Jin, Nattapol Chanpaisit, et al. Vbench: Comprehensive benchmark suite for video generative\n1022\t  models. In Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition, pp.\n1023\t  21807–21818, 2024.\n1024\t\n1025\tChunyu Li, Jiaye Li, Ruiqiao Mei, Haoyuan Xia, Hao Zhu, Jingdong Wang, and Siyu Zhu. Hallo-live: Real-\n1026\t  time streaming joint audio-video avatar generation with asynchronous dual-stream and human-centric\n1027\t  preference distillation. arXiv preprint arXiv:2604.23632, 2026a.\n1028\t\n1029\t\n1030\t                                                      15\n1031\t\fVorch-Streamer: Extending Human Audio-Visual Generation to Real-Time Long-Form Streaming\n1032\t\n1033\t\n1034\t\n1035\t\n1036\tHaoran Li, Fredreic Li, Shichen Ma, Jie Huang, Yijun Liu, Jiaqi Shi, and Yanwen Ma. Joyai-echo: Pushing\n1037\t  the frontier of long audio-visual generation. 2026b.\n1038\t\n1039\tYixin Liu, Kai Zhang, Yuan Li, Zhiling Yan, Chujie Gao, Ruoxi Chen, Zhengqing Yuan, Yue Huang, Hanchi\n1040\t  Sun, Jianfeng Gao, et al. Sora: A review on background, technology, limitations, and opportunities of\n1041\t  large vision models. arXiv preprint arXiv:2402.17177, 2024.\n1042\t\n1043\tChetwin Low, Weimin Wang, and Calder Katyal. Ovi: Twin backbone cross-modal fusion for audio-video\n1044\t  generation. arXiv preprint arXiv:2510.01284, 2025.\n1045\t\n1046\tXiang Lyu, Yuxuan Wang, Tianyu Zhao, Hao Wang, Huadai Liu, and Zhihao Du. Build llm-based zero-shot\n1047\t  streaming tts system with cosyvoice. In ICASSP 2025-2025 IEEE International Conference on Acoustics,\n1048\t  Speech and Signal Processing (ICASSP), pp. 1–2. IEEE, 2025.\n1049\t\n1050\tXin Ma, Yaohui Wang, Xinyuan Chen, Gengyun Jia, Ziwei Liu, Yuan-Fang Li, Cunjian Chen, and Yu Qiao.\n1051\t  Latte: Latent diffusion transformer for video generation. Transactions on Machine Learning Research,\n1052\t  2025a.\n1053\t\n1054\tXin Ma, Yaohui Wang, Gengyun Jia, Xinyuan Chen, Tien-Tsin Wong, Yuan-Fang Li, and Cunjian Chen.\n1055\t  Consistent and controllable image animation with motion diffusion models. In Computer Vision and\n1056\t  Pattern Recognition, pp. 7288–7298, 2025b.\n1057\t\n1058\tXin Ma, Yaohui Wang, Genyun Jia, Xinyuan Chen, Tien-Tsin Wong, and Cunjian Chen. Consistent and\n1059\t  controllable image animation with motion linear diffusion transformers. IEEE Transactions on Pattern\n1060\t  Analysis and Machine Intelligence, 2026.\n1061\t\n1062\tAlec Radford, Jong Wook Kim, Chris Hallacy, Aditya Ramesh, Gabriel Goh, Sandhini Agarwal, Girish\n1063\t  Sastry, Amanda Askell, Pamela Mishkin, Jack Clark, et al. Learning transferable visual models from\n1064\t  natural language supervision. In International conference on machine learning, pp. 8748–8763. PmLR,\n1065\t  2021.\n1066\t\n1067\tTeam Seedance, De Chen, Liyang Chen, Xin Chen, Ying Chen, Zhuo Chen, Zhuowei Chen, Feng Cheng,\n1068\t  Tianheng Cheng, Yufeng Cheng, et al. Seedance 2.0: Advancing video generation for world complexity.\n1069\t  arXiv preprint arXiv:2604.14148, 2026.\n1070\t\n1071\tLe Shen, Qian Qiao, Tan Yu, Ke Zhou, Tianhang Yu, Yu Zhan, Zhenjie Wang, Ming Tao, Shunshun Yin,\n1072\t  and Siyuan Liu. SoulX-FlashTalk: Real-time infinite streaming of audio-driven avatars via self-correcting\n1073\t  bidirectional distillation, 2025. URL https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.23379.\n1074\t\n1075\tYaofeng Su, Yuming Li, Zeyue Xue, Jie Huang, Siming Fu, Haoran Li, Ying Li, Zezhong Qian, Haoyang\n1076\t  Huang, and Nan Duan. Omniforcing: Unleashing real-time joint audio-visual generation. arXiv preprint\n1077\t  arXiv:2603.11647, 2026.\n1078\t\n1079\tZhiyao Sun, Ziqiao Peng, Yifeng Ma, Yi Chen, Zhengguang Zhou, Zixiang Zhou, Guozhen Zhang, Youliang\n1080\t  Zhang, Yuan Zhou, Qinglin Lu, et al. Streamavatar: Streaming diffusion models for real-time interactive\n1081\t  human avatars. In Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition,\n1082\t  pp. 10887–10897, 2026.\n1083\t\n1084\tLinrui Tian, Qi Wang, and Bang Zhang. Streamchar: Long-horizon streaming character audio-video gener-\n1085\t  ation with decoupled orchestration. arXiv preprint arXiv:2605.25659, 2026.\n1086\t\n1087\tThomas Unterthiner, Sjoerd Van Steenkiste, Karol Kurach, Raphael Marinier, Marcin Michalski, and Syl-\n1088\t  vain Gelly. Towards accurate generative models of video: A new metric & challenges. arXiv preprint\n1089\t  arXiv:1812.01717, 2018.\n1090\t\n1091\tYaohui Wang, Xinyuan Chen, Xin Ma, Shangchen Zhou, Ziqi Huang, Yi Wang, Ceyuan Yang, Yinan He,\n1092\t  Jiashuo Yu, Peiqing Yang, et al. Lavie: High-quality video generation with cascaded latent diffusion\n1093\t  models. International Journal of Computer Vision, pp. 1–20, 2024.\n1094\t\n1095\t\n1096\t                                                    16\n1097\t\fVorch-Streamer: Extending Human Audio-Visual Generation to Real-Time Long-Form Streaming\n1098\t\n1099\t\n1100\t\n1101\t\n1102\tYaohui Wang, Xin Ma, Xinyuan Chen, Cunjian Chen, Antitza Dantcheva, Bo Dai, and Yu Qiao. Leo:\n1103\t  Generative latent image animator for human video synthesis. International Journal of Computer Vision,\n1104\t  133(3):1277–1289, 2025.\n1105\tShuai Yang, Wei Huang, Ruihang Chu, Yicheng Xiao, Yuyang Zhao, Xianbang Wang, Muyang Li, Enze\n1106\t  Xie, Yingcong Chen, Yao Lu, et al. Longlive: Real-time interactive long video generation. arXiv preprint\n1107\t  arXiv:2509.22622, 2025.\n1108\t\n1109\tTianwei Yin, Michaël Gharbi, Richard Zhang, Eli Shechtman, Fredo Durand, William T Freeman, and\n1110\t  Taesung Park. One-step diffusion with distribution matching distillation. In Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF\n1111\t  conference on computer vision and pattern recognition, pp. 6613–6623, 2024.\n1112\tTianwei Yin, Qiang Zhang, Richard Zhang, William T Freeman, Fredo Durand, Eli Shechtman, and Xun\n1113\t  Huang. From slow bidirectional to fast autoregressive video diffusion models. In Proceedings of the\n1114\t  IEEE/CVF Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition, pp. 22963–22974, 2025.\n1115\tDian Zheng, Ziqi Huang, Hongbo Liu, Kai Zou, Yinan He, Fan Zhang, Lulu Gu, Yuanhan Zhang, Jingwen He,\n1116\t  Wei-Shi Zheng, et al. Vbench-2.0: Advancing video generation benchmark suite for intrinsic faithfulness.\n1117\t  arXiv preprint arXiv:2503.21755, 2025.\n1118\t\n1119\tHongzhou Zhu, Min Zhao, Guande He, Hang Su, Chongxuan Li, and Jun Zhu. Causal forcing: Autoregressive\n1120\t  diffusion distillation done right for high-quality real-time interactive video generation. arXiv preprint\n1121\t  arXiv:2602.02214, 2026.\n1122\t\n1123\t\n1124\t\n1125\t\n1126\t                                                    17\n1127\t\f\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "4bc4cbc5-e3da-422e-a8fe-e89ffc2571cb", "uuid": "0195cc61-74b1-4ad9-8608-3be47e369b28", "timestamp": "2026-08-07T06:00:27.301Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/vorch_streamer.txt", "content": "                                        Vorch-Streamer: Extending Human Audio-Visual Generation to Real-Time Long-Form Streaming\n\n\n\n\n                                        Vorch-Streamer: Extending Human Audio-Visual Genera-\n                                        tion to Real-Time Long-Form Streaming\n\n                                        Menglin Han1,2∗ , Yang Ding1∗ , Yulei Lu1 , Haoran Yu1,3 , Xin Ma1 , Junyi Chen1,4 ,\n                                        Zhangkai Ni2† , Lin Ma† , Yaohui Wang1†\n                                        1\n                                            Vorch Team 2 Tongji University 3 Harbin Institute of Technology, Shenzhen 4 Shanghai Jiao Tong University\n\n\n\n\n                                                                          Real-Time Long-Form Audio-Video Joint Generation @ 27 FPS\n                                                                        0s               30s               60s               90s         Last frame (~120s)\n                                              This video shows\n                                             a strong, middle-\n                                               aged white male\n                                              in his thirties,\n                                             against a wooden…\narXiv:2608.05663v1 [cs.CV] 6 Aug 2026\n\n\n\n\n                                              What it does is it\n                                            causes a prompt drop …\n\n\n                                              Realistic indoor\n                                             kitchen scene. The\n                                            camera is positioned\n                                               in an eye-level\n                                               close-up shot …\n\n                                              Mmm. That tastes so\n                                               damn good. These …\n\n\n                                               Realistic indoor\n                                               vlog-style video\n                                              featuring a young\n                                                 woman sharing\n                                             product recommend…\n\n                                               Actually, I have\n                                            noticed lately that I…\n\n\n\n\n                                        Figure 1: Real-time long-form text-to-audio-video generation with Vorch-Streamer. Conditioned on a\n                                        global caption and long speech (left), our model causally and jointly streams video and synchronized speech\n                                        at 27 FPS, without externally supplied audio or a reference first frame. Frames sampled at 0, 30, 60, and\n                                        90 seconds and near the end of three approximately two-minute rollouts demonstrate sustained identity and\n                                        scene consistency together with expressive facial, gestural, and object motion; the blue waveforms visualize\n                                        the continuously generated audio.\n\n                                                                                               Abstract\n\n                                                      Real-time long-form avatar audio–video generation requires causal, continuous synthesis\n                                                      while maintaining audiovisual synchronization and visual consistency. Adapting a pretrained\n                                                      bidirectional model to this setting presents two key dilemmas. First, autoregressively reusing\n                                                      generated blocks as context creates exposure bias, causing errors and visual drift to accu-\n                                                      mulate over long rollouts. Second, a global speech utterance does not indicates a causal\n                                                      generator which portion should be spoken next when only limited local audio–video context\n                                                      is available. We present Vorch-Streamer, a post-training framework that addresses these\n                                                      challenges and enables real-time long-form Text-to-Audio-Video (T2AV) streaming. We con-\n                                                      struct a synthetic corpus of 80K avatar clips spanning 12–21 seconds and first train a causal\n                                                      generator with mixed Teacher Forcing and Diffusion Forcing. We then apply long-horizon\n                                                      Self Forcing with DMD distillation, exposing the model to its own rollout distribution while\n                                        *Equal contribution †Corresponding author.\n\n\n                                                                                                    1\n\fVorch-Streamer: Extending Human Audio-Visual Generation to Real-Time Long-Form Streaming\n\n\n\n\n        preserving the quality of the pretrained bidirectional teacher. To explicitly control speech\n        progression, an external language model predicts discrete 25-Hz speech-planning tokens,\n        whose continuous features condition the audio diffusion branch and align each causal block\n        with the content it should speak. With bounded causal context and four-step denoising,\n        Vorch-Streamer jointly generates audio and video from text at 27.12 FPS, exceeding the\n        24-FPS real-time playback rate while maintaining competitive audio–lip synchronization\n        and strong identity preservation over long-form generation. Our project page is available at\n        https://vorch-project.github.io/Vorch-Streamer-project/.\n\n\n1   Introduction\nReal-time avatar audio-video generation is a key capability for education, entertainment, virtual communi-\ncation, and interactive agents. Modern audio-video diffusion models support diverse conditioning modes,\nincluding text-, image-, and audio-conditioned generation, and can synthesize visually expressive faces to-\ngether with natural speech (Liu et al., 2024; HaCohen et al., 2026; Seedance et al., 2026). However, most of\nthem are designed for short, offline clips. Their bidirectional attention patterns assume that the complete\nsequence is available during denoising, which is incompatible with a streaming system that must generate\nthe next audio-video segment before future content is observed. Extending these models to long-form real-\ntime generation therefore requires more than reducing inference latency: the model must operate causally,\npreserve audio-video synchronization over an extended horizon, and remain stable when its own previous\npredictions become the context for subsequent blocks (Huang et al., 2026c; Yin et al., 2025; Zhu et al., 2026).\nThis setting exposes two related dilemmas. First, long-form streaming requires the model to roll out many\nblocks autoregressively, feeding its own predictions back as context. Small errors can therefore accumulate\nover time, creating a train-test mismatch between clean training conditions and self-generated inference\nhistories, i.e., the well-known exposure-bias problem. Under causal attention, this distribution shift can\ncause compounding artifacts, temporal drift, and unstable long-horizon generation. Second, Text-to-Audio-\nVideo (T2AV) exposes a mismatch between global speech conditioning and causal audio-video generation.\nThe text prompt describes the entire utterance, whereas each streaming block can attend only to a limited\nwindow of previously generated audio-video content. With bidirectional attention, every token can use\nthe full text and sequence context, but under causal attention each block must infer which portion of the\nutterance it should generate next from limited context alone. This is manageable for short clips but becomes\na major bottleneck in long-form streaming, especially when the complete transcript is unavailable in advance\nor when speech is supplied in frequently switched short segments. Without explicit speech planning, the\ngenerated audio may start from the middle of the intended speech rather than its beginning, or otherwise\nfall out of temporal alignment with the corresponding speech position.\nWe present Vorch-Streamer, a post-training framework that extends the pretrained LTX2.3 audio-video\nfoundation model to real-time, long-form streaming. Vorch-Streamer separates the transition to causal\ngeneration from the transition to long-horizon generation. We first construct a synthetic corpus of 80K\nhigh-quality avatar audio-video clips with durations of 12-21 seconds. Subsequently, a causal autoregressive\nstreaming model is trained using a sample-level mixture of Teacher Forcing and Diffusion Forcing, which\nbehaves as a model capable of 20-step causal short-form streaming with class-free guidance (CFG). The\nresulting short-form causal model is further optimized with long-horizon Self Forcing. In this stage, the\ncausal model performs self-rollouts over 12-21 second horizons with 4-step denoising, while the original\nbidirectional LTX2.3 acts as the teacher for distillation. This combination exposes the student to the\ndistribution it will encounter at inference time and transfers the quality of the pretrained model to long\ncausal trajectories. Together, the causal training and long-horizon distillation transform a bidirectional\ndiffusion model into a stable streaming generator with 4-step causal long-form streaming, which solves the\nfirst dilemma of exposure-bias.\nTo address the second dilemma of speech progression under a limited attention window, Vorch-Streamer\nintroduces an explicit speech-planning pathway based on the Fun-CosyVoice LLM (Lyu et al., 2025). The\nplanner predicts discrete speech-planning tokens, each corresponding to a 40 ms planning unit. We extend\nthe pretrained lookup table (LUT) with a learnable silence token and convert the resulting token IDs into\n\n\n                                                      2\n\fVorch-Streamer: Extending Human Audio-Visual Generation to Real-Time Long-Form Streaming\n\n\n\n\ncontinuous planning features with this LUT. These features are injected into the audio branch through an\nadditional cross-attention branch and adaptively fused with the original text-to-audio cross-attention output\nusing a gated fusion operator. This decoupling of speech planning from audio generation assigns content\nand scheduling to the planner while allowing the generative model to focus on synchronized audio-video\nrealization. It also allows an ongoing utterance to be interrupted or switched to new speech at any time\nduring streaming, and the learnable silence token gives the model an explicit silent-listening capability,\nproviding a foundation for interactive applications.\nExperiments show that Vorch-Streamer is the only evaluated native T2AV method to exceed the 24-FPS\nreal-time playback rate, while retaining competitive audio–lip synchronization and spoken-content accuracy\nover long-form generation. The results demonstrate that post-training, explicit speech planning, and causal\ninference provide a practical route from a powerful bidirectional foundation model to a real-time text-driven\navatar generator.\nOur contributions are summarized as follows:\n\n      • We introduce Vorch-Streamer, a post-training framework that adapts a pretrained bidirectional\n        audio-video diffusion model to causal, real-time, long-form streaming generation.\n      • We construct an 80K synthetic avatar audio-video corpus and combine mixed Teacher Forc-\n        ing/Diffusion Forcing with long-horizon Self Forcing and teacher distillation to align causal training\n        with streaming inference.\n      • We propose an LLM-based speech-planning pathway for T2AV that supplies continuous planning\n        features to the audio branch, supports interruption and speech switching through the decoupling of\n        planning and generation, and introduces a learnable silence token for silent listening.\n      • Experiments demonstrate real-time, long-form T2AV streaming at 27.12 FPS, together with com-\n        petitive synchronization, speech accuracy, and long-horizon identity preservation.\n\n2     Related Work\n\n2.1   Joint Audio-Video Generation\n\nEarly audio-visual generation systems commonly synthesize video and audio in a cascade, which does not\ndirectly model their joint distribution and may accumulate semantic and temporal errors across stages.\nEarly text-to-video generation models focus on improving the quality of the generated videos (Wang et al.,\n2024; 2025; Chen et al., 2024; Ma et al., 2025b; 2026). Latte proposed a latent Diffusion Transformer\narchitecture, laying out a scalable Transformer blueprint that has informed all subsequent video foundation\nmodels (Ma et al., 2025a). Recent text-to-audio-video models instead generate both modalities in a unified\nprocess. Ovi (Low et al., 2025) employs symmetric twin diffusion-transformer backbones with block-wise\nbidirectional cross-modal fusion, while LTX-2 (HaCohen et al., 2026) adopts an asymmetric dual-stream\narchitecture coupled through bidirectional audio-video cross-attention. Both models can jointly synthesize\nspeech, ambient sound, and corresponding visual events from text, and LTX-2 provides the foundation model\nused by Vorch-Streamer.\nDespite their strong audio-visual quality, these models are designed primarily for bounded offline generation.\nOvi focuses on short clips, while LTX-2 reports temporal drift and degraded synchronization beyond its\nsupported duration. More fundamentally, both of them rely on full-sequence bidirectional denoising, pre-\nventing incremental streaming output and efficient KV-cache reuse. Enabling streaming generation therefore\nrequires converting their temporal dependency from bidirectional to causal rather than merely reducing the\nnumber of sampling steps.\n\n2.2   Real-Time and Long-Form Video Generation\n\nReal-time streaming generation requires causal temporal modeling, few-step sampling, and stability under\nrepeated conditioning on self-generated history. CausVid and Self Forcing (Yin et al., 2025; Huang et al.,\n\n\n                                                      3\n\fVorch-Streamer: Extending Human Audio-Visual Generation to Real-Time Long-Form Streaming\n\n\n\n\n2026c) establish representative bidirectional-to-causal distillation pipelines, with Self Forcing explicitly re-\nducing the train–test mismatch through autoregressive self-rollout. Causal Forcing (Zhu et al., 2026) further\nidentifies an architectural mismatch when initializing a causal student from a bidirectional teacher and in-\nstead uses an autoregressive teacher for ODE initialization. However, it is a text-to-video method evaluated\non approximately five-second clips, and does not establish long-horizon or joint audio-video stability.\nLongLive (Yang et al., 2025) combines streaming long tuning, short-window causal attention, a persistent\nframe sink, and KV recaching to support real-time long-form generation and online prompt changes. It\ndemonstrates the importance of train-long–test-long adaptation, but remains a silent T2V model and there-\nfore does not address speech generation or cross-modal synchronization. OmniForcing (Su et al., 2026) is\nthe closest general-purpose precursor for causal joint audio-video generation. It distills the bidirectional\ndual-stream LTX-2 model into a streaming autoregressive generator using asymmetric audio-video block\nalignment, audio sink tokens, joint Self-Forcing, and rolling KV caches. However, its quantitative evaluation\nis conducted on short audio-video clips, and it does not study long-form avatar speech, transcript progression,\nor speech interruption.\nVorch-Streamer instead focuses on long-horizon joint generation and explicitly addresses the mismatch be-\ntween a global speech specification and the limited local context available to a causal generator.\n\n\n\n\n2.3   Human-Centric Audio-Video Generation\n\n\nHuman-centric generation additionally requires identity preservation, accurate lip synchronization, expressive\nmotion, and stability throughout long interactions. Live Avatar (Huang et al., 2025) and StreamAvatar (Sun\net al., 2026) adapt large human video diffusion models into causal streaming generators and introduce\npersistent reference mechanisms to reduce long-term identity drift. They support long or interactive avatar\nrendering, including talking and listening behaviors, but both depend on externally supplied driving audio.\nStreamAvatar further disables text control in its interactive model. Consequently, these methods do not\njointly determine and synthesize speech, and require an upstream dialogue or speech-generation system.\nHallo-Live (Li et al., 2026a) and StreamChar (Tian et al., 2026) move toward real-time text-driven joint\naudio-video avatars. Hallo-Live introduces asynchronous dual-stream diffusion with limited future audio\ncontext and human-centric preference-guided distillation, but its published evaluation focuses on short-form\ngeneration and leaves longer conversations to future work. StreamChar supports long-form streaming through\nan LLM orchestrator that reads the transcript and audio history, followed by a joint audio-video DiT for local\ndenoising. Its long-form protocol, however, supplies a complete global transcript in advance and does not\nreport interrupting or replacing the active speech during generation. It also requires task-specific pretraining\nof the language orchestrator and substantial joint adaptation of a Wan-derived audio-video backbone. Vorch-\nStreamer instead reuses a pretrained speech language model to provide planning features to a post-trained\ncausal backbone, allowing speech to be interrupted or switched without fixing the complete future utterance\nat the beginning of a stream.\nJoyAI-Echo (Li et al., 2026b) uses paired cross-modal memory to preserve appearance and voice consistency\nacross minute-scale multi-shot audio-video stories. Its released pipeline operates on a pre-authored sequence\nof complete shot descriptions, and the public results do not establish online block-wise generation under a\nreal-time playback budget. It is therefore better characterized as accelerated multi-shot long-form generation\nthan causal continuous streaming. Wan-Streamer (Huang et al., 2026b;a) takes a broader, natively causal\napproach by modeling language, audio, and video inputs and outputs in one Transformer, enabling full-duplex\nperception, speaking, listening, and interruption, which is trained as a new end-to-end interactive foundation\nmodel with causal encoders and decoders, broad multimodal pretraining, duplex interaction training, and a\nspecialized serving system. Its public reports emphasize interaction latency but do not provide standardized\nminute-scale generation-quality evaluations. Vorch-Streamer addresses a complementary setting by post-\ntraining an existing high-quality bidirectional audio-video foundation model for causal long-form streaming\nwhile retaining its pretrained generative capabilities.\n\n\n                                                       4\n\fVorch-Streamer: Extending Human Audio-Visual Generation to Real-Time Long-Form Streaming\n\n\n\n\n       Stage 1: Synthetic Corpus              Stage 2: Mixed TF/DF Training                     Stage 3: Long-Horizon Self Forcing\n                                                                                                            causal window\n         Human-Centric Prompts             History sequence Denoise sequence                                  KV Cache\n\n\n                                                                          causal                      Long-Horizon Rollout\n       Pretrain T2AV model                     Causal                      attn.\n                                                                                                                      block denoise\n                                                 base                       mask\n                                                model\n          80K synthetic clips\n          12-21s audio-video samples         Flow-matching\n                                                  Loss                                                Long-Horizon DMD Loss\n\n                                              LLM-based Speech Planning\n                            LLM Speech      SP tokens              Embedding LUT      SP embeddings\n          Speech             Planner\n          Utterance\n                                                    Time-aligned clip\n                                                                                                            sigmoid\n                                                                                                             gate\n                                                                                                  s2a\n                                                Audio feature                                   output\n                                                tokens\n                                                                                    s2a attn.\n                                Text\n                              Encoder      Text embedding\n                                               tokens                                                                        refined\n           Global                                                                                 t2a\n           Caption                                                                              output\n                                                                                                              ⊕             t2a output\n                                                                          t2a attn.\n\n\n\n                                         Figure 2: Overview of Vorch-Streamer.\n\n\n3     Method\n\nVorch-Streamer extends the pretrained LTX2.3 audio–video diffusion model to real-time, long-form avatar\naudio–video joint generation. As illustrated in Fig. 2, our method contains three post-training stages.\nWe first construct a large-scale synthetic avatar corpus using the pretrained bidirectional LTX2.3 model.\nSubsequently, a mixed training strategy composed by Diffusion Forcing and Teacher Forcing is adopted to\nconvert the bidirectional model into a block-autoregressive streaming generator. Finally, long-horizon self\nforcing aligns the model with its inference-time rollout distribution under the supervision of the original\nbidirectional model. We additionally use the Fun-CosyVoice LLM as an explicit speech planner, resolving\nthe mismatch between global textual conditioning and causal audio generation by intergrating the planning\nunits into audio branch with cross-attention and gated injection.\n\n3.1   Synthetic Avatar Data\n\nLarge-scale, high-quality, and temporally aligned audio–video data are essential for adapting a foundation\nmodel to streaming avatar generation. We therefore use the pretrained LTX2.3 model to synthesize 80K\nhigh-quality avatar audio–video clips with durations ranging from 12 to 21 seconds. Because the samples\nare generated by the same foundation model used for initialization, they provide model-consistent audio–\nvideo supervision for post-training. The resulting corpus is used for both causal streaming training and\nlong-horizon self forcing.\n\n3.2   Causal Audio–Video Streaming\n\nSynchronized block generation. Let x0v and x0a denote clean video and audio latents, respectively.\nVorch-Streamer partitions them into a sequence of synchronized audio–video blocks,\n\n                                              Bb = (x0v [svb : evb ], x0a [sab : eab ]) ,                                                (1)\n\nwhere b is the block index. The VAE of LTX2.3 represents video at 3 latent frames per second and audio\nat 25 latent tokens per second. Accordingly, each regular block contains three video latent frames and 25\naudio latent tokens, corresponding to approximately one second of generated content. Due to the causal\n\n\n                                                                  5\n\fVorch-Streamer: Extending Human Audio-Visual Generation to Real-Time Long-Form Streaming\n\n\n\n\nVAE alignment, the first block contains one additional video frame and one additional audio token, following\nOmniForcing (Su et al., 2026).\nWe preserve bidirectional attention within each block to model fine-grained audio–video interactions, while\nenforcing causal attention across blocks. To provide both stable identity context and recent motion context,\nblock b attends to the first three blocks and the most recent three preceding blocks during training,\n\n                                 Hb = {0, 1, 2} ∪ {max(0, b − 3), . . . , b − 1},                           (2)\n\nbut keep adjustable for inference. This bounded global-local window enables constant-memory streaming\nwithout discarding the initial appearance and scene information, while maintaining stable memory con-\nsumption. During causal training, the dependency is imposed by causal window attention masks. During\nlong-form training and inference rollout, the same dependency is implemented with a windowed KV cache\nand cache eviction.\n\nMixed Teacher Forcing and Diffusion Forcing. Directly applying Teacher Forcing exposes the model\nonly to clean ground-truth histories, whereas streaming inference conditions on previously generated and\npotentially imperfect blocks. To reduce this train–test discrepancy, we train the causal model with a sample-\nlevel mixture of Teacher Forcing and Diffusion Forcing. For modality m ∈ {v, a}, a clean latent is corrupted\nthrough flow-matching interpolation,\n\n                                 xσm = (1 − σ)x0m + σϵm ,        ϵm ∼ N (0, I).                             (3)\n\nEach sample is organized as a dual-sequence input containing a history sequence and a denoising sequence\nwith same content. For 10% of the samples, the history sequence remains clean, corresponding to Teacher\nForcing. For the remaining 90%, the history sequence is corrupted, corresponding to Diffusion Forcing.\nThe causal mask allows the current denoising block to access preceding history blocks but prevents it from\nreading the clean target of the current block, as shown in Fig. 2.\nThe model is optimized with the standard audio–video flow-matching objective,\n                                         X                          2\n                                 LCF =        λm ∥bvm − (ϵm − x0m )∥2 .                                     (4)\n                                           m∈{v,a}\n\n\nThis stage transfers the pretrained model’s joint audio–video generation ability to a causal architecture and\nproduces a short-form streaming model that serves as the initialization for long-horizon training.\n\n3.3   Long-Horizon Self Forcing\n\nAlthough mixed causal forcing makes the model robust to locally corrupted context, it does not fully repro-\nduce the structured errors accumulated during autoregressive inference. Over long sequences, small errors\nin appearance, motion, speech, or synchronization can propagate through the causal history and eventually\ncause severe quality degradation. We address this problem with long-horizon self forcing.\nStarting from the causal model obtained above, the student generates a 12–21 second sequence block by\nblock from its own predictions. Each new block is conditioned on the student’s previously generated blocks\nthrough the same bounded KV-cache window used at inference time. Consequently, training directly exposes\nthe model to its own long-horizon rollout distribution, closing the exposure gap between causal training and\nstreaming inference. The fixed-window cache also prevents memory from growing with sequence length,\nmaking the training behavior consistent with real-time deployment.\nWe retain the original bidirectional LTX2.3 model as a frozen teacher and use distribution matching dis-\ntillation (DMD) (Yin et al., 2024) to preserve the quality and semantic fidelity of the pretrained model.\nThe DMD framework contains three networks with complementary roles: the causal student produces long-\nhorizon rollouts; the frozen bidirectional real-score model represents the target distribution of the pretrained\nLTX2.3 model; and a trainable bidirectional fake-score model estimates the evolving distribution of the\nstudent’s generated samples by flow-matching loss.\n\n\n                                                        6\n\fVorch-Streamer: Extending Human Audio-Visual Generation to Real-Time Long-Form Streaming\n\n\n\n\n                        e0m , we first perturb it to a random noise level,\nGiven a student rollout x\n                                           eσm = (1 − σ)e\n                                           x            x0m + σϵm .                                        (5)\nThe same noisy audio–video sample is then evaluated by the real-score and fake-score models,\n                                                       m\n                                     pm                  xσv , x\n                                      real = real_score (e     eσa , c, σ) ,                               (6)\n                                                       m\n                                     pm                  xσv , x\n                                      fake = fake_score (e     eσa , c, σ) .                               (7)\nFor the real-score network, conditional and unconditional predictions are combined to form the guided\nteacher target, while the fake-score network is evaluated on the conditional student distribution. Thus,\npmreal indicates how the frozen bidirectional teacher would denoise the sample toward the pretrained data\ndistribution, whereas pmfake describes the current student distribution. Their difference provides the DMD\nupdate direction,\n                                              gm ∝ pm        m\n                                                     fake − preal .                                     (8)\nThe causal student is optimized using the corresponding stop-gradient surrogate\n                                          X λm                           2\n                               LDMD =              ∥e         x0m − gm )∥2 ,\n                                                    x0m − sg (e                                            (9)\n                                                 2\n                                          m∈{v,a}\n\nwhich moves the student samples toward the real-score prediction and away from the distribution currently\ncaptured by the fake-score model.\nThe fake-score model is updated separately on detached student rollouts. It is trained with the standard\naudio–video flow-matching objective,\n                                         X                             2\n                                                    m\n                               Lfake =        λm ∥bvfake − (ϵm − x\n                                                                 e0m )∥2 .                          (10)\n                                          m∈{v,a}\n\nAlternating the student and fake-score updates continually refreshes the estimate of the student distribution,\nallowing the DMD direction to remain informative as the causal generator improves.\n\n3.4   LLM-based Speech Planning\n\nStreaming audio–video avatar generation introduces an additional challenge for text conditioning. A conven-\ntional text prompt contains the complete speech transcript, but a causal block must determine which portion\nof the transcript should be spoken at the current time. This alignment is implicit in a bidirectional model,\nwhere all text, audio, and video tokens can interact globally. Under causal attention, however, the same\nprompt leaves the diffusion model responsible for deciding both what to say and when to say it. The ambi-\nguity becomes particularly problematic in long-form generation and near the boundaries between successive\nspeech segments.\nVorch-Streamer explicitly separates speech planning from audio–video realization. Given the target utter-\nance, the planning LLM predicts a sequence of discrete speech-planning tokens\n                                               z = (z1 , . . . , zTa ),                                  (11)\nat 25 Hz, where each token represents a 40-ms speech-planning unit. The tokens are converted into continuous\nfeatures using the pretrained Fun-CosyVoice lookup table followed by a learnable projection,\n                                             est = fproj (ELUT [zt ]) .                                  (12)\nBecause the planning sequence and the LTX audio latents share the same 25-Hz temporal resolution, each\ncausal block receives the speech features associated with its own time interval.\nWe inject these planning features into the audio diffusion branch through an additional speech cross-attention\nmodule, and strict the audio tokens to only attend the planning features within the same block. Its output\nis adaptively fused with the original text-conditioned audio features using a learnable gate,\n                                 hℓa ← hℓa + sigmoid(gℓ ) Attnℓspeech (hℓa , es ) ,                      (13)\n\n\n                                                          7\n\fVorch-Streamer: Extending Human Audio-Visual Generation to Real-Time Long-Form Streaming\n\n\n\n\nwhere hℓa is the audio hidden state and gℓ is the gate in layer ℓ. The original text cross-attention remains\nunchanged and continues to describe global semantics, appearance, and speaking style, while the speech\nbranch supplies an explicit local speaking schedule. This division allows the diffusion model to focus on\nsynchronized audio–video generation rather than inferring speech timing from a global transcript.\nDuring the post-training of Vorch-Streamer, the causal student is conditioned on the global scene description\nand the aligned speech-planning tokens. The bidirectional teacher in Self Forcing still receives prompts\nwith full utterance through its original text-conditioning pathway. This asymmetric conditioning allows the\nteacher to retain its pretrained global modeling ability while supervising a student designed for causal speech\nexecution.\n\n4     Experiments\n\nWe conduct quantitative and qualitative comparisons against existing joint and cascaded avatar generation\nsystems, evaluating generation quality, audio–video synchronization, speech accuracy, inference speed, and\nstability over approximately two-minute generations. We further ablate the speech-planning module, the\nStage 2 and Stage 3 training strategy, long-horizon Self Forcing, and causal-context selection.\n\n4.1   Experimental Setup\n\nImplementation details. We initialize Vorch-Streamer from the 22B-parameter LTX2.3 model and train\nit on 80K synthetic avatar clips lasting 12–21 seconds at a resolution of 768 × 512 and 24 FPS. Stage 2 uses\n10% Teacher Forcing and 90% Diffusion Forcing and is trained for 6,000 steps on 64 NVIDIA H200 GPUs\nwith Fully Sharded Data Parallel (FSDP), bfloat16 mixed precision, gradient checkpointing, and a global\nbatch size of 64 with learning rate 1e − 4. Stage 3 initializes the causal student from the Stage 2 checkpoint\nand performs self-rollouts over the full 12–21-second training horizon. The original bidirectional LTX2.3\nis kept frozen as the real-score teacher for distribution matching distillation. We train Stage 3 for 1,000\niterations on 32 NVIDIA H200 GPUs with a global batch size of 32 and a critic-to-generator update ratio\nof 6:1, with learning rate 1e − 5 for both generator and fake-score model. At inference time, Vorch-Streamer\nuses four denoising steps for each approximately one-second audio–video block. Unless otherwise specified,\nthe causal window uses the 3+1 setting selected by the context ablation: three persistent prefix blocks and\nthe most recent preceding block.\n\nEvaluation protocol. We construct a held-out benchmark containing diverse human identities, languages,\nspeaking styles, backgrounds, and utterance lengths. No prompt in the benchmark appears in the synthetic\ntraining corpus. All comparative evaluations are performed on continuous long-form rollouts targeting ap-\nproximately two minutes. Aggregate visual metrics are computed over the complete available rollout, while\ntime-dependent metrics are sampled at a fixed one-second interval to reveal error accumulation.\nThe T2AV task requires a generator to synthesize both speech and video directly from text. Vorch-Streamer\nis evaluated only in this native T2AV setting. For reference, the “+TTS” baselines are evaluated as TIA2V\npipelines: they receive speech generated by Qwen3-TTS and a first frame generated by LTX2.3 before video\nsynthesis begins. These results are useful conditional-generation references, but they are not directly task-\nequivalent to from-scratch T2AV generation because the avatar generator is given both the target waveform\nand an appearance anchor. Since many systems lack explicit frame control, determining output length via\naudio or internal mechanisms, we target a nominal two-minute duration. Note that actual lengths may vary.\nOmniForcing and Hallo-Live are limited to 30 seconds due to OOM errors; their temporal curves terminate\nat these respective durations, as indicated in the tables.\n\nBaselines. We compare against the bidirectional foundation model LTX2.3 (HaCohen et al., 2026), the\njoint audio–video generators JoyAI-Echo (Li et al., 2026b), the streaming avatar model Hallo-Live (Li et al.,\n2026a) and OmniForcing (Su et al., 2026), and two cascaded TIA2V reference pipelines, LiveAvatar (Huang\net al., 2025)+TTS and SoulX-FlashTalk (Shen et al., 2025)+TTS. LTX2.3 provides a strong offline T2AV\nquality reference but cannot emit blocks causally. JoyAI-Echo, OmniForcing, Hallo-Live, and Vorch-Streamer\nare evaluated in their native joint T2AV setting. LiveAvatar and SoulX-FlashTalk are included only as\n\n\n                                                      8\n\fVorch-Streamer: Extending Human Audio-Visual Generation to Real-Time Long-Form Streaming\n\n\n\n\nTable 1: Quantitative comparison on continuous long-form avatar generation. “Task” distinguishes native\njoint T2AV generation from TIA2V reference pipelines driven by Qwen3-TTS audio and an LTX2.3-generated\nfirst frame. “Stream” indicates causal block-wise output. Quality metrics are aggregated over the complete\navailable rollout. Best results are shown in bold and second-best results are underlined; T2AV and TIA2V\nresults should be interpreted under their different input requirements.\n                  Method                   #Param.    Task    Stream   FPS ↑   Sync-C ↑   Sync-D ↓   WER ↓\n                  LTX2.3                     22B      T2AV             1.83      6.80       7.96     7.59%\n                  JoyAI-Echo                 22B      T2AV             3.08      2.44      12.91     9.34%\n                  LiveAvatar+TTS†            14B     TIA2V       ✓     7.58     7.18       8.30      9.21%\n                  SoulX-FlashTalk+TTS†      18.9B    TIA2V       ✓     5.11     8.69       7.26      9.21%\n                  OmniForcing (30 s)         19B      T2AV       ✓     12.12     0.75      12.22     98.79%\n                  Hallo-Live (30 s)         11.7B     T2AV       ✓     11.51     0.58      13.90     94.13%\n                  Vorch-Streamer (ours)     22.8B     T2AV       ✓     27.12     6.62      8.95      7.92%\n †\n  Conditional TIA2V reference using Qwen3-TTS audio and an LTX2.3-generated first frame. Its WER is determined by the shared\n TTS input and does not measure the avatar generator itself.\n\n\n\nconditional references and generate video from the common Qwen3-TTS (Hu et al., 2026) audio and the\nLTX2.3-generated first frame; Vorch-Streamer does not use either auxiliary input.\n\nMetrics. We report DiT throughput in frames per second (FPS), calculated as generated frames divided by\nend-to-end wall-clock time on one NVIDIA H200 GPU. The quantitative comparison employs nine metrics:\nSync-C (↑) and Sync-D (↓) for audio–lip synchronization (Chung & Zisserman, 2016); FID (↓) and FVD\n(↓) (Heusel et al., 2017; Unterthiner et al., 2018) for visual quality; VBench (Huang et al., 2024) Dynamic\nDegree (↑) and Drift (↓); VBench2 (Zheng et al., 2025) Human Anatomy (↑) and Identity (↑); and word\nerror rate (WER, ↓). Besides, we assess temporal consistency via ArcFace (Deng et al., 2019) and CLIP\nimage similarities (Radford et al., 2021) relative to the initial frame. ArcFace computes cosine similarity on\naligned face embeddings to measure identity preservation, while CLIP uses full-frame embeddings for global\nappearance consistency.\n\n4.2   Quantitative Comparison\n\nEnd-to-end generation quality and efficiency. Table 1 compares Vorch-Streamer with existing joint\nand cascaded avatar generation systems. Vorch-Streamer reaches 27.12 FPS with a 22.8B-parameter pipeline,\nmaking it the only evaluated native T2AV model that exceeds the 24-FPS real-time playback rate in one\nH200 GPU. It is 8.8× faster than JoyAI-Echo, 14.8× faster than bidirectional LTX2.3, approximately 2.2×\nfaster than OmniForcing, and approximately 2.4× faster than Hallo-Live. Among native T2AV methods,\nVorch-Streamer obtains a WER of 7.92%, close to the offline LTX2.3 result of 7.59%. In contrast, directly\nextrapolating OmniForcing and Hallo-Live produces WERs of 98.79% and 94.13%, showing that their speech\nlargely deviates from the target transcript. This result reflects the second dilemma: without explicit speech\nplanning, causal blocks cannot reliably determine which part of the global transcript should be spoken\nnext. Vorch-Streamer’s speech planner preserves transcript accuracy over the approximately two-minute\ngeneration. Its Sync-C/Sync-D scores of 6.62/8.95 are also close to LTX2.3 at 6.80/7.96, showing that\nfour-step causal inference preserves most of the synchronization and speech accuracy of the much slower\nbidirectional model.\nThe TIA2V references achieve strong synchronization on some metrics, particularly SoulX-FlashTalk+TTS\nwith Sync-C 8.69 and Sync-D 7.26. However, this advantage comes from a substantially easier input setting:\nboth TIA2V pipelines are supplied with the complete target audio and a generated first frame, so they\nneither generate the audiovisual content from scratch nor determine speech progression from text. They also\nrun at only 7.58 and 5.11 FPS in one GPU, respectively, both far below real-time playback and 3.6×–5.3×\nslower than Vorch-Streamer. Their WER is inherited from the shared Qwen3-TTS input and therefore does\nnot measure the avatar generator. More importantly, external audio bypasses rather than solves the second\ndilemma: the pipeline no longer has to align a global text instruction with the locally available causal audio\ncontext.\n\n\n                                                             9\n\fVorch-Streamer: Extending Human Audio-Visual Generation to Real-Time Long-Form Streaming\n\n\n\n\nTable 2: Visual quality and human-centric fidelity over continuous long-form rollouts. Scores are aggregated\nuniformly over temporal positions in the complete available output.\n  Method                  FID ↓   FVD ↓ Dynamic ↑ Drift ↓ Human Anatomy ↑ Human Identity ↑ ArcFace ↑ CLIP I.Sim. ↑\n  LTX2.3                  115.12 521.74     0.2941      0.0776        0.9680             0.8733         0.5679       0.8866\n  JoyAI-Echo              110.81 354.85     0.0471      0.0481        0.9710             0.9789         0.6777       0.9075\n  LiveAvatar+TTS      120.94      457.85    0.2824    0.0301          0.9651             0.9722         0.7387       0.9532\n  SoulX-FlashTalk+TTS 123.13      448.33    0.2000    0.0231          0.9719             0.9849         0.8431       0.9552\n  OmniForcing (30 s)      167.26 1217.10    0.1529      0.0663        0.8884            0.8012          0.3870       0.8804\n  Hallo-Live (30 s)       143.18 1007.90    0.0000      0.0811        0.9291            0.8670          0.4652       0.8752\n  Vorch-Streamer (ours)   119.80 549.99     0.2706      0.0286        0.9233            0.9996          0.7534       0.9324\n\n\n\nTable 3: Long-horizon preservation over each method’s available rollout. We report the score over the final\n10-second window and, in parentheses, its change from the initial 10-second window.\nMethod                    Sync-C ↑         Sync-D ↓          Dynamic ↑             Drift ↓        Human Anatomy ↑ Human Identity ↑\nLTX2.3              6.21 (−18.51%) 8.46 (+14.98%) 0.1647 (−61.11%) 0.0336 (−32.93%)                0.9589 (−1.21%)   0.9595 (+0.07%)\nLiveAvatar+TTS      7.04 (−3.57%)   8.38 (+2.80%)   0.2941 (−3.85%) 0.0234 (−17.54%)               0.9707 (+0.32%)   0.9960 (+1.12%)\nSoulX-FlashTalk+TTS 8.79 (+1.65%)   7.25 (−1.55%)   0.2118 (+28.57%) 0.0202 (−8.99%)               0.9690 (+0.16%)   0.9956 (+1.22%)\nJoyAI-Echo          2.24 (−16.30%) 12.90 (+0.11%) 0.0353 (−50.00%) 0.0355 (+68.78%)                0.9868 (+2.19%)   0.9874 (−1.13%)\nOmniForcing (30 s)  0.46 (−31.94%) 13.52 (+2.40%) 0.2235 (+280.00%) 0.0096 (−78.35%)               0.9117 (+3.44%)   1.0000 (+13.04%)\nHallo-Live (30 s)   0.29 (−53.94%) 15.58 (+12.05%) 0.0000 (−100.00%) 0.0243 (−30.41%)              0.9000 (−3.76%)   0.9633 (−0.70%)\nVorch-Streamer (ours)   6.67 (−0.49%)   8.88 (+0.47%)     0.2588 (+10.00%)     0.0221 (−5.48%)     0.9284 (−0.04%)   1.0000 (+0.06%)\n\n\n\n\nVisual quality and human fidelity. Table 2 reports perceptual quality and human fidelity aggregated\nover each method’s complete available long-form rollout. Vorch-Streamer achieves the highest Human Iden-\ntity score (0.9996), the second-highest Dynamic Degree among native T2AV methods (0.2706), and the\nsecond-lowest Drift overall (0.0286), while maintaining competitive FID and FVD. SoulX-FlashTalk obtains\nhigher ArcFace similarity, and both TIA2V references obtain higher CLIP similarity. This is unsurprising\nbecause their first-frame condition directly fixes the target identity and scene appearance. The conditional\nadvantage should not be interpreted as stronger from-scratch generation: Vorch-Streamer must create the\ninitial person, voice, motion, and scene jointly from text and then preserve them causally. Compared with\nthe native streaming T2AV baselines, Vorch-Streamer is markedly stronger in FID, FVD, dynamics, drift,\nidentity, ArcFace, and CLIP similarity, while its anatomy score remains comparable; OmniForcing and\nHallo-Live are additionally evaluated only up to 30 seconds.\n\n4.3   Long-Horizon Analysis\n\nWe evaluate long-horizon behavior from both endpoint changes and complete temporal trajectories. Table 3\nreports each metric over the final 10-second window together with its relative change from the initial 10\nseconds. Vorch-Streamer exhibits little endpoint degradation over its approximately two-minute rollout.\nMeanwhile, its Dynamic Degree remains high at 0.2588 and increases by 10.00%, showing that the stable\nappearance is not achieved by producing a static video. In comparison, the native T2AV baselines show\nsubstantially larger long-horizon changes. LTX2.3 loses 18.51% in Sync-C and 61.11% in Dynamic Degree,\nand JoyAI-Echo loses 16.30% and 50.00%, respectively, while its Drift increases by 68.78%. OmniForcing\nand Hallo-Live already suffer marked synchronization degradation within their shorter 30-second outputs,\nwith Hallo-Live also losing all measured dynamics. The TIA2V references preserve strong endpoint metrics,\nbut they are conditioned on both external audio and a first frame and therefore serve as conditional refer-\nences rather than like-for-like T2AV comparisons. Overall, Table 3 shows that Vorch-Streamer provides the\nstrongest balance of speech synchronization, sustained motion, and long-term preservation among the native\nstreaming T2AV methods.\nFig. 3 complements the endpoint comparison by showing when appearance degradation occurs. For each\nvideo, we compute ArcFace and CLIP Image cosine similarities between the first frame and the frame sam-\npled at each subsequent second, and then average the independently computed trajectories across benchmark\nsamples; the curves begin at t = 1 second, show 95% confidence intervals, and terminate at each method’s ac-\ntual maximum duration without extrapolation. Vorch-Streamer remains stable throughout its approximately\n\n\n                                                                 10\n\fVorch-Streamer: Extending Human Audio-Visual Generation to Real-Time Long-Form Streaming\n\n\n\n\n                                   ArcFace similarity to the first frame                                                                                   CLIP Image similarity to the first frame\n                 1.0                                                                                                      1.00\n\n\n                                                                                                                      0.95\n\n                 0.8\n                                                                                                                      0.90\n  ＞土」e-\n\n\n\n\n                                                                                                            >1\n                                                                                                                          0\n                                                                                                                              郖\n                                                                                                             le-\n                 0\n                 6\n\n\n\n\n                                                                                                            」  l\n      lE1\n\n\n\n\n                                                                                                                E1\n                                                                                                                          0\n                                                                                                                          80 75\n        sau1\n\n\n\n\n                                                                                                                 sau1\n                 0\n                 4\n           s0u\n\n\n\n\n                                                                                                                    sou\n                                                                                                                             0\n                                                                                                                      0.70\n                 0.2\n                                                                                                                      0.65\n\n\n                 0.0                                                                                                  0.60\n                            20     40             60                80           100            120                                             20            40              60                80    100   120\n                                          Time from first frame (s)                                                                                                   Time from first frame (s)\n\n                                                                         一   Hal lo-Live   一   LTX23           一              OmniForcing        一   Vorch-Streamer\n                                                                         —   丿oyAI-Ech。    —   LiveAvatar      —              SoulX-FlashTalk\n\n\n\n\nFigure 3: Long-horizon consistency as a function of generation time. The two panels show ArcFace similarity\nand CLIP Image similarity to the first frame of the same video. ArcFace measures facial-identity preservation,\nwhereas CLIP Image measures broader appearance and scene preservation. Solid lines denote the mean\nacross benchmark samples and shaded regions denote 95% confidence intervals. Curves terminate at the\nmaximum duration completed by each method and are never extrapolated. VBench Dynamic Degree is\nreported separately in Table 2 to rule out artificially high similarity from static outputs.\n\n\n\n\ntwo-minute rollout: after the initial transition, ArcFace similarity stays around 0.73–0.77 and CLIP Image\nsimilarity around 0.92–0.94, with no sustained downward trend. In contrast, the native T2AV methods that\ncomplete long rollouts degrade continuously: LTX2.3 falls to roughly 0.42 ArcFace and 0.82 CLIP similarity,\nwhile JoyAI-Echo falls to roughly 0.62 and 0.88, respectively. OmniForcing and Hallo-Live also decline before\nterminating at 30 seconds. The TIA2V curves remain strong, particularly for SoulX-FlashTalk, but reflect\nthe additional identity and content anchors supplied by their first-frame and audio conditions. Together, the\nendpoint metrics and temporal curves show that Vorch-Streamer maintains identity and scene appearance\nthroughout long native T2AV generation rather than merely delaying degradation beyond a short evaluation\nwindow.\n\n\n\n\n4.4                    Qualitative Comparison\n\n\nFig. 4 compares two long-form prompts at the beginning, middle, and end of each available rollout. Vorch-\nStreamer preserves the generated subject’s facial structure, clothing, and background over 120 and 113\nseconds, while still changing gaze, expression, hand pose, and object orientation. This combination is\nimportant: a nearly static video can score well on appearance similarity without demonstrating useful long-\nform generation, whereas the Vorch-Streamer samples remain visibly dynamic.\nThe native T2AV baselines exhibit different limitations. LTX2.3 develops severe facial and background\ncorruption near the end, while Hallo-Live already contains strong color and structural artifacts by 30 seconds.\nJoyAI-Echo remains visually coherent and includes a clear hand–object interaction in the second example,\nbut its left sequence shows relatively little pose variation, while exhibit noticeable degradation in faces.\nOmniForcing exhibits noticeable frame freezing and substantial visual quality degradation within just 30\nseconds of generation, indicating poor temporal stability even over relatively short horizons. The TIA2V\nrows preserve identity more reliably, but they begin from the same externally generated first frame and follow\nexternally supplied audio. Their visual stability therefore reflects strong conditional animation rather than\nfrom-scratch audiovisual generation, and it does not demonstrate a solution to text-to-speech progression\nunder a causal context. Overall, the qualitative comparison supports the quantitative finding that Vorch-\nStreamer offers a better balance of long-horizon stability, motion, and real-time native T2AV generation.\n\n\n                                                                                                      11\n\fVorch-Streamer: Extending Human Audio-Visual Generation to Real-Time Long-Form Streaming\n\n\n\n\n                     1s            60s             120s              1s              60s             113s\n\n\n      LTX2.3\n\n\n\n\n  JoyAI-Echo\n\n\n\n\n   LiveAvatar\n\n\n\n\n       SoulX-\n      FlashTalk\n\n\n\n   Hallo-Live\n  (1 / 15 / 30s)\n\n\n\n  OmniForcing\n  (1 / 15 / 30s)\n\n\n\n Vorch-Streamer\n     (Ours)\n\n\n\nFigure 4: Qualitative comparison on two long-form T2AV prompts. We show frames near the beginning,\nmiddle, and end of each available rollout; OmniForcing and Hallo-Live are limited to 30 seconds and are\ntherefore sampled at 1, 15, and 30 seconds. LiveAvatar and SoulX-FlashTalk are TIA2V references condi-\ntioned on Qwen3-TTS audio and an LTX2.3-generated first frame, whereas all other rows synthesize audio\nand video directly from text. Vorch-Streamer maintains coherent identity and scene structure throughout\nthe full sequence while retaining visible facial and upper-body motion.\n\n\n4.5      Ablation Study\n\n4.5.1      Effect of Speech Planning\n\nWe compare the proposed speech-planning features with three alternatives that expose text to the audio\ngenerator with different degrees of temporal localization. As shown in Table 4, conditioning every block on\nthe complete utterance produces an extremely high WER of 184%. The same global transcript is visible\nthroughout generation, but the causal generator has no reliable signal indicating which part should be spoken\nin the current block, directly exposing the second dilemma described in the Introduction. In practice, it\ngenerates unnaturally fast and incorrect speech as it attempts to realize the long utterance from limited local\naudio context. Splitting the utterance into short text segments reduces the visible attention and lowers WER\nto 62.77%, since the generator only needs to select content from a small piece of text. However, when the\ncondition switches to a new segment, the cached audio context still corresponds to the preceding segment\nand is inconsistent with the new text. This mismatch can make generation enter the new segment from\nan intermediate position and can also cause repeated speech. Assigning a timestamp to every text token,\nencoding it with RoPE, and restricting each audio token to its corresponding text window further alleviates\nthis boundary ambiguity. However, consecutive word repetitions still occur with non-negligible probability,\nintroducing insertion errors and keeping WER relatively high.These designs do not fundamentally resolve the\n\n\n                                                      12\n\fVorch-Streamer: Extending Human Audio-Visual Generation to Real-Time Long-Form Streaming\n\n\n\n\nTable 4: Ablation of speech planning for T2AV. WER measures spoken-content accuracy, while Sync-C and\nSync-D measure audio–lip synchronization.\n                            Speech condition          WER ↓ Sync-C ↑ Sync-D ↓\n                            Complete long utterance 184%          5.58      9.66\n                            Segmented short text    62.77%        6.76      8.98\n                            Window text RoPE        65.46%        4.75      9.19\n                            LLM speech plan (ours)   7.92%        6.62      8.95\n\n\n\nTable 5: Ablation of Stage 2 and Stage 3 on continuous long-form rollouts. Without Stage 2, the bidirectional\nDiT is first flow-matching fine-tuned to use the introduced LLM planner, after which causal audio–video\nforcing is skipped and Stage 3 is applied. Without Stage 3, the Stage 2 checkpoint is evaluated using 20-step\nCFG denoising; the full model uses four denoising steps per block. Dynamic Degree and Drift are computed\nwith VBench, Human Identity with VBench2, and WER measures spoken-content accuracy.\n   Variant                         FVD ↓ Sync-C ↑ Sync-D ↓ Dynamic ↑ Drift ↓ Human Identity ↑ WER ↓\n   Without Stage 2               515.30        6.89    8.73      0.0824    0.0238       1.0000       11.16%\n   Without Stage 3 (20-step CFG) 442.99        6.07    9.18      0.3059    0.0312       0.9939       9.17%\n   Stage 2 + Stage 3 (ours)      549.99        6.62    8.95      0.2706    0.0286       0.9996       7.92%\n\n\n\nsecond dilemma. In contrast, the LLM planner converts the utterance into an explicit block-aligned sequence\nof speech units, reducing WER to 7.92% while retaining synchronization comparable to other conditioning\nstrategies. These results show that temporal restrictions on text attention are helpful, but explicitly planning\nwhat should be spoken at each time step is crucial for accurate causal T2AV generation.\n\n4.5.2   Effect of Stage 2 and Stage 3\n\nWe ablate Stage 2 and Stage 3 to isolate the necessity of causal initialization before long-horizon Self Forcing\nand the benefit of adapting the resulting causal generator to efficient few-step inference. To ensure that the\nspeech-planning condition is effective in a fair “Without Stage 2” comparison, we first introduce the LLM\nplanner and fine-tune the bidirectional DiT with the flow-matching objective. We then skip the causal audio–\nvideo forcing in Stage 2 and initialize Stage 3 from this planner-adapted bidirectional checkpoint. Table 5\nshows that Stage 2 is essential for establishing a causal generator with meaningful motion before long-horizon\nSelf Forcing. Applying Stage 3 without this causal initialization reduces Dynamic Degree from 0.2706 to\n0.0824, while WER increases slightly from 7.92% to 11.16%. Although this variant obtains comparable\nsynchronization and seemingly favorable Drift and Human Identity scores, the temporal-preservation metrics\nmust be interpreted together with its severe loss of dynamics: a low-motion rollout is less likely to accumulate\nvisual drift or identity changes. Stage 2 therefore provides more than adaptation to the speech planner; it\npreserves the motion-generation capability while converting the bidirectional model into a causal generator\nsuitable for meaningful long-form rollout. Stage 3 plays a complementary role. Relative to the Stage 2\ncheckpoint evaluated with 20-step CFG, it enables four-step inference while improving Sync-C/Sync-D from\n6.07/9.18 to 6.62/8.95, WER from 9.17% to 7.92%, Drift from 0.0312 to 0.0286, and Human Identity from\n0.9939 to 0.9996. Together, the two stages prevent motion collapse while adapting the causal generator to\naccurate and efficient few-step long-form inference.\n\n4.5.3   Effect of Long-Horizon Self Forcing\n\nTo isolate the benefit of training on complete causal trajectories, we compare Stage 2 alone with self forcing\nrestricted to the first five, a random five, or the last five blocks, as well as the proposed full-horizon self\nforcing. Restricting the objective to a small part of the rollout produces unbalanced behavior. Training\non the first five blocks yields the highest Dynamic Degree (0.5059), but also substantially increases Drift\nto 0.0739 and reduces Human Identity to 0.9021, indicating that the additional motion is accompanied by\naccumulated appearance changes. Supervising only the last five blocks is even less stable, with the highest\n\n\n                                                      13\n\fVorch-Streamer: Extending Human Audio-Visual Generation to Real-Time Long-Form Streaming\n\n\n\n\nTable 6: Ablation of long-horizon self forcing. FVD, VBench Dynamic Degree and Drift, and VBench2\nHuman Identity are computed over the available rollout. ArcFace and CLIP-I report the mean similarity of\nthe final 10-second window to the first frame.\n         Training horizon              FVD ↓ Dynamic ↑ Drift ↓ Human Identity ↑ ArcFace ↑ CLIP-I ↑\n         Stage 2 only (20-step CFG)    442.99    0.3059    0.0312       0.9939            0.7478     0.9445\n         First five blocks             556.32    0.5059    0.0739       0.9021            0.5800     0.8757\n         Random five blocks            461.95    0.1647    0.0698       0.9878            0.6869     0.8861\n         Last five blocks              443.87    0.2000    0.1368       0.6995            0.4437     0.7895\n         Full horizon (ours)           549.99    0.2706    0.0286       0.9996            0.7534     0.9324\n\n\nTable 7: Inference-time ablation of causal-context selection. Following Table 6, FVD, VBench Dynamic\nDegree and Drift, and VBench2 Human Identity are computed over the available rollout. ArcFace and\nCLIP-I report the mean similarity of the final 10-second window to the first frame.\n                 Context      FVD ↓ Dynamic ↑ Drift ↓ Human Identity ↑ ArcFace ↑ CLIP-I ↑\n                 1+3          544.13    0.2941    0.0383       0.9328            0.5882     0.9082\n                 0+3          501.70    0.2235    0.1589       0.6921            0.2759     0.7533\n                 3+3          498.93    0.2706    0.0377       0.9427            0.6386     0.9150\n                 3+1 (ours)   549.99    0.2706    0.0286       0.9996            0.7534     0.9324\n\n\n\nDrift (0.1368) and the lowest identity and final-window similarities, because the late blocks must recover from\nearlier self-generated states that were not directly optimized. Random-five-block training covers more varied\nstates, but its Drift (0.0698) remains more than twice that of full-horizon training. Full-horizon self forcing\nachieves the lowest Drift (0.0286), the highest Human Identity (0.9996), and the highest ArcFace similarity\n(0.7534). Although it does not minimize FVD or maximize Dynamic Degree, it provides the strongest overall\nlong-horizon preservation, supporting the need to optimize the complete inference trajectory rather than a\nsparse subset of rollout positions.\n\n4.5.4   Impact of Context Selection\n\nWe evaluate context selection as an inference-time ablation, keeping the model fixed and varying only the\ncausal attention window. We denote a window by P +R, where P is the number of persistent prefix blocks\nand R is the number of recent blocks; the Stage 2 checkpoint with 20-step CFG in Table 6 is reported only as a\nreference. The 3+1 setting is comparable to the other context variants in metrics such as FVD and Dynamic\nDegree, but its necessity becomes clear over long rollouts. All other settings exhibit varying degrees of\ndegradation, most clearly in Drift, ArcFace, and CLIP-I. Without a persistent prefix, 0+3 degrades severely\nto 0.1589 Drift, 0.2759 ArcFace, and 0.7533 CLIP-I. Retaining prefix context in 1+3 and 3+3 alleviates the\nfailure, but their Drift remains higher at 0.0383 and 0.0377, respectively, and their ArcFace/CLIP-I scores\nremain lower at 0.5882/0.9082 and 0.6386/0.9150. In contrast, 3+1 achieves the lowest Drift of 0.0286 and the\nhighest ArcFace and CLIP-I scores of 0.7534 and 0.9324 among the tested context windows. These results\nmake the 3+1 context necessary for reliable long-video inference: three persistent prefix blocks maintain\nglobal appearance anchors, while using only the immediately preceding block as recent context limits the\naccumulation of errors from self-generated history. Its advantage lies primarily in long-horizon preservation\nrather than uniformly improving the other quality metrics.\n\n5   Conclusion\n\nWe presented Vorch-Streamer, a post-training framework that transforms a pretrained bidirectional audio–\nvideo diffusion model into a causal generator for real-time, long-form text-to-audio-video avatar generation.\nCausal audio–video forcing provides a stable initialization for synchronized block-wise generation, and long-\nhorizon self forcing exposes the model to its own rollout distribution while using the frozen bidirectional\nmodel to preserve generation quality. An LLM-based speech planner aligns global textual content with the\n\n\n                                                          14\n\fVorch-Streamer: Extending Human Audio-Visual Generation to Real-Time Long-Form Streaming\n\n\n\n\ncausal audio timeline, improving the model’s ability to determine what should be spoken in each block. To-\ngether with a bounded global–local context and four-step block denoising, these components enable efficient\nstreaming without allowing memory consumption to grow with sequence length. Experiments on continu-\nous, approximately two-minute generations show that Vorch-Streamer reaches 27.12 FPS while maintaining\ncompetitive visual quality, audio–lip synchronization, speech accuracy, and strong long-term identity preser-\nvation. Unlike the conditional TIA2V references, it requires neither an input audio track nor a first frame and\ndirectly addresses speech progression from text under causal generation. These results establish causal forcing\nand inference-aligned long-horizon training as a practical route for adapting large bidirectional audio–video\nfoundation models to interactive avatar generation.\n\nReferences\nXinyuan Chen, Yaohui Wang, Lingjun Zhang, Shaobin Zhuang, Xin Ma, Jiashuo Yu, Yali Wang, Dahua\n  Lin, Yu Qiao, and Ziwei Liu. Seine: Short-to-long video diffusion model for generative transition and\n  prediction. In International Conference on Learning Representations, 2024.\n\nJoon Son Chung and Andrew Zisserman. Out of time: automated lip sync in the wild. In Asian conference\n  on computer vision, pp. 251–263. Springer, 2016.\n\nJiankang Deng, Jia Guo, Niannan Xue, and Stefanos Zafeiriou. Arcface: Additive angular margin loss\n  for deep face recognition. In Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF conference on computer vision and pattern\n  recognition, pp. 4690–4699, 2019.\n\nYoav HaCohen, Benny Brazowski, Nisan Chiprut, Yaki Bitterman, Andrew Kvochko, Avishai Berkowitz,\n  Daniel Shalem, Daphna Lifschitz, Dudu Moshe, Eitan Porat, et al. Ltx-2: Efficient joint audio-visual\n  foundation model. arXiv preprint arXiv:2601.03233, 2026.\n\nMartin Heusel, Hubert Ramsauer, Thomas Unterthiner, Bernhard Nessler, and Sepp Hochreiter. Gans\n trained by a two time-scale update rule converge to a local nash equilibrium. Advances in neural infor-\n mation processing systems, 30, 2017.\n\nHangrui Hu, Xinfa Zhu, Ting He, Dake Guo, Bin Zhang, Xiong Wang, Zhifang Guo, Ziyue Jiang, Hongkun\n  Hao, Zishan Guo, et al. Qwen3-tts technical report. arXiv preprint arXiv:2601.15621, 2026.\n\nLianghua Huang, Zhi-Fan Wu, Yupeng Shi, Wei Wang, Mengyang Feng, Junjie He, Chen-Wei Xie, Yu Liu,\n  Jingren Zhou, Ang Wang, et al. Wan-streamer v0. 2: Higher resolution, same latency. arXiv preprint\n  arXiv:2607.04443, 2026a.\n\nLianghua Huang, Zhifan Wu, Wei Wang, Yupeng Shi, Mengyang Feng, Junjie He, Chenwei Xie, Yu Liu,\n  Jingren Zhou, Ang Wang, et al. Wan-streamer v0. 1: End-to-end real-time interactive foundation models.\n  arXiv preprint arXiv:2606.25041, 2026b.\n\nXun Huang, Zhengqi Li, Guande He, Mingyuan Zhou, and Eli Shechtman. Self forcing: Bridging the\n train-test gap in autoregressive video diffusion. Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems, 38:\n 167283–167308, 2026c.\n\nYubo Huang, Hailong Guo, Fangtai Wu, Weiqiang Wang, Shifeng Zhang, Shijie Huang, Qijun Gan, Lin Liu,\n  Sirui Zhao, Enhong Chen, et al. Live avatar: Streaming real-time audio-driven avatar generation with\n  infinite length. arXiv preprint arXiv:2512.04677, 2025.\n\nZiqi Huang, Yinan He, Jiashuo Yu, Fan Zhang, Chenyang Si, Yuming Jiang, Yuanhan Zhang, Tianxing Wu,\n  Qingyang Jin, Nattapol Chanpaisit, et al. Vbench: Comprehensive benchmark suite for video generative\n  models. In Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition, pp.\n  21807–21818, 2024.\n\nChunyu Li, Jiaye Li, Ruiqiao Mei, Haoyuan Xia, Hao Zhu, Jingdong Wang, and Siyu Zhu. Hallo-live: Real-\n  time streaming joint audio-video avatar generation with asynchronous dual-stream and human-centric\n  preference distillation. arXiv preprint arXiv:2604.23632, 2026a.\n\n\n                                                      15\n\fVorch-Streamer: Extending Human Audio-Visual Generation to Real-Time Long-Form Streaming\n\n\n\n\nHaoran Li, Fredreic Li, Shichen Ma, Jie Huang, Yijun Liu, Jiaqi Shi, and Yanwen Ma. Joyai-echo: Pushing\n  the frontier of long audio-visual generation. 2026b.\n\nYixin Liu, Kai Zhang, Yuan Li, Zhiling Yan, Chujie Gao, Ruoxi Chen, Zhengqing Yuan, Yue Huang, Hanchi\n  Sun, Jianfeng Gao, et al. Sora: A review on background, technology, limitations, and opportunities of\n  large vision models. arXiv preprint arXiv:2402.17177, 2024.\n\nChetwin Low, Weimin Wang, and Calder Katyal. Ovi: Twin backbone cross-modal fusion for audio-video\n  generation. arXiv preprint arXiv:2510.01284, 2025.\n\nXiang Lyu, Yuxuan Wang, Tianyu Zhao, Hao Wang, Huadai Liu, and Zhihao Du. Build llm-based zero-shot\n  streaming tts system with cosyvoice. In ICASSP 2025-2025 IEEE International Conference on Acoustics,\n  Speech and Signal Processing (ICASSP), pp. 1–2. IEEE, 2025.\n\nXin Ma, Yaohui Wang, Xinyuan Chen, Gengyun Jia, Ziwei Liu, Yuan-Fang Li, Cunjian Chen, and Yu Qiao.\n  Latte: Latent diffusion transformer for video generation. Transactions on Machine Learning Research,\n  2025a.\n\nXin Ma, Yaohui Wang, Gengyun Jia, Xinyuan Chen, Tien-Tsin Wong, Yuan-Fang Li, and Cunjian Chen.\n  Consistent and controllable image animation with motion diffusion models. In Computer Vision and\n  Pattern Recognition, pp. 7288–7298, 2025b.\n\nXin Ma, Yaohui Wang, Genyun Jia, Xinyuan Chen, Tien-Tsin Wong, and Cunjian Chen. Consistent and\n  controllable image animation with motion linear diffusion transformers. IEEE Transactions on Pattern\n  Analysis and Machine Intelligence, 2026.\n\nAlec Radford, Jong Wook Kim, Chris Hallacy, Aditya Ramesh, Gabriel Goh, Sandhini Agarwal, Girish\n  Sastry, Amanda Askell, Pamela Mishkin, Jack Clark, et al. Learning transferable visual models from\n  natural language supervision. In International conference on machine learning, pp. 8748–8763. PmLR,\n  2021.\n\nTeam Seedance, De Chen, Liyang Chen, Xin Chen, Ying Chen, Zhuo Chen, Zhuowei Chen, Feng Cheng,\n  Tianheng Cheng, Yufeng Cheng, et al. Seedance 2.0: Advancing video generation for world complexity.\n  arXiv preprint arXiv:2604.14148, 2026.\n\nLe Shen, Qian Qiao, Tan Yu, Ke Zhou, Tianhang Yu, Yu Zhan, Zhenjie Wang, Ming Tao, Shunshun Yin,\n  and Siyuan Liu. SoulX-FlashTalk: Real-time infinite streaming of audio-driven avatars via self-correcting\n  bidirectional distillation, 2025. URL https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.23379.\n\nYaofeng Su, Yuming Li, Zeyue Xue, Jie Huang, Siming Fu, Haoran Li, Ying Li, Zezhong Qian, Haoyang\n  Huang, and Nan Duan. Omniforcing: Unleashing real-time joint audio-visual generation. arXiv preprint\n  arXiv:2603.11647, 2026.\n\nZhiyao Sun, Ziqiao Peng, Yifeng Ma, Yi Chen, Zhengguang Zhou, Zixiang Zhou, Guozhen Zhang, Youliang\n  Zhang, Yuan Zhou, Qinglin Lu, et al. Streamavatar: Streaming diffusion models for real-time interactive\n  human avatars. In Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition,\n  pp. 10887–10897, 2026.\n\nLinrui Tian, Qi Wang, and Bang Zhang. Streamchar: Long-horizon streaming character audio-video gener-\n  ation with decoupled orchestration. arXiv preprint arXiv:2605.25659, 2026.\n\nThomas Unterthiner, Sjoerd Van Steenkiste, Karol Kurach, Raphael Marinier, Marcin Michalski, and Syl-\n  vain Gelly. Towards accurate generative models of video: A new metric & challenges. arXiv preprint\n  arXiv:1812.01717, 2018.\n\nYaohui Wang, Xinyuan Chen, Xin Ma, Shangchen Zhou, Ziqi Huang, Yi Wang, Ceyuan Yang, Yinan He,\n  Jiashuo Yu, Peiqing Yang, et al. Lavie: High-quality video generation with cascaded latent diffusion\n  models. International Journal of Computer Vision, pp. 1–20, 2024.\n\n\n                                                    16\n\fVorch-Streamer: Extending Human Audio-Visual Generation to Real-Time Long-Form Streaming\n\n\n\n\nYaohui Wang, Xin Ma, Xinyuan Chen, Cunjian Chen, Antitza Dantcheva, Bo Dai, and Yu Qiao. Leo:\n  Generative latent image animator for human video synthesis. International Journal of Computer Vision,\n  133(3):1277–1289, 2025.\nShuai Yang, Wei Huang, Ruihang Chu, Yicheng Xiao, Yuyang Zhao, Xianbang Wang, Muyang Li, Enze\n  Xie, Yingcong Chen, Yao Lu, et al. Longlive: Real-time interactive long video generation. arXiv preprint\n  arXiv:2509.22622, 2025.\n\nTianwei Yin, Michaël Gharbi, Richard Zhang, Eli Shechtman, Fredo Durand, William T Freeman, and\n  Taesung Park. One-step diffusion with distribution matching distillation. In Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF\n  conference on computer vision and pattern recognition, pp. 6613–6623, 2024.\nTianwei Yin, Qiang Zhang, Richard Zhang, William T Freeman, Fredo Durand, Eli Shechtman, and Xun\n  Huang. From slow bidirectional to fast autoregressive video diffusion models. In Proceedings of the\n  IEEE/CVF Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition, pp. 22963–22974, 2025.\nDian Zheng, Ziqi Huang, Hongbo Liu, Kai Zou, Yinan He, Fan Zhang, Lulu Gu, Yuanhan Zhang, Jingwen He,\n  Wei-Shi Zheng, et al. Vbench-2.0: Advancing video generation benchmark suite for intrinsic faithfulness.\n  arXiv preprint arXiv:2503.21755, 2025.\n\nHongzhou Zhu, Min Zhao, Guande He, Hang Su, Chongxuan Li, and Jun Zhu. Causal forcing: Autoregressive\n  diffusion distillation done right for high-quality real-time interactive video generation. arXiv preprint\n  arXiv:2602.02214, 2026.\n\n\n\n\n                                                    17\n\f", "numLines": 1127, "startLine": 1, "totalLines": 1127}}}
